window.PHYSICS_SOURCES=[{"id": "m1-main", "module": 1, "title": "Fluids & buoyancy", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 1 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "b7791a1a038a54a1ea357e5373528d881aba0ae0518fbf4e29b431b293b18545", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Fluid Mechanics\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department │FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Density and Pressure\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department │FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "Archimedes’ Principle\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department │FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Density and Pressure\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department │FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDefine and apply the concepts of density and fluid pressure to solve physical\nproblems.\n§\nDefine and apply concepts of absolute, gauge, and atmospheric pressures.\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "FLUID MECHANICS is the branch of science which deals \nwith the study of the behavior of fluids (gases or liquids)\nFLUID is any substance that can flow either a liquid or a \ngas\nFLUID STATICS  is the study of fluids at rest or equilibrium \nsituations\nFLUID DYNAMICS is the study of fluids in motion.\nDEFINITION OF TERMS\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "DENSITY is defined as mass per unit volume.\nA homogenous material such as iron or ice has some density ρ\nthroughout.\nDEFINITION OF TERMS\n𝝆= 𝒎\n𝑽\nWhere:\nm = mass (kg)\nV = volume (m3)\nFigure 1. Two objects with different masses and different\nvolumes but the same density. From. Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics(pp373) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "DENSITIES OF SOME COMMON SUBSTANCES\nTable 1. Densities of Some Common Substances. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics(pp374) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R.\nA. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "RELATIVE DENSITY\nThe relative density ρr of a material is the ratio of \nits density to the density of water (1000 kg/m3).\nSteel (7800 kg/m3)\nρr = 7.80\nBrass (8700 kg/m3)\nρr = 8.70\nWood (500 kg/m3)\nρr = 0.500\n3\n1000 kg/m\nx\nr\nr\nr =\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "SAMPLE\n1. Find the mass and weight of the air at 200C in a \nliving room with a 4.0 m by 5.0 m and a ceiling at 3.0 m \nhigh. \nSolution\nV = (4.0m)(5.0m)(3.0) = 60 m3\nmair = ρairV = (1.20 kg/m3)(60 m3) \nmair = 72 kg\nwair= mairg = (72kg)(9.81m/s2) = 706.32 N\nwair= 706.32 N\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "PRESSURE\nIt is the amount of force \nexerted on a given area. \nIts SI unit is Pascal (Pa)\nIf the applied force is acting on a small \narea, then the pressure will be large.\n𝑃= 𝐹\n𝐴\nWhere:\nP = pressure ( Pa)\nF = force in Newton (N)\nA = area (m2)\nUnit: Pascal (Pa)\n1Pa = 1 N/m2\nFigure 2 Pressure Formula . From. https://www.testo.com/en-IN/services/knowledgebase-pressure-physical-\nprinciples\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "Fluid Pressure\nSuppose we have a tank filled with fluid with density ρ.\nThe tank is filled up to a height “h” with a cross sectional area \n“A”\n𝑷= 𝝆𝒈𝒉\nWhere\nρ = density  (Pa)\ng = gravitational \nacceleration (m/s2)\nh = height (m)\nAs you go deeper the swimming pool, the \npressure gets higher\nFigure 3. Tank filled with fluid. From. https://www.slideshare.net/reastment/pressure-in-fluids\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "FLUID PRESSURE\nA liquid or gas cannot sustain a shearing stress - it is only \nrestrained by a boundary.  Thus, it will exert a force \nagainst and perpendicular to that boundary.\nThe force F exerted by a fluid on \nthe walls of its container always \nacts perpendicular to the walls.\nFigure 4.  Fluid exerted by a fluid on  the walls.. From. \nhttps://www.slideshare.net/nosuhaila/chapter2-24683465\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "FLUID PRESSURE\nFluid exerts forces in many directions. Try to \nsubmerse a rubber ball in water to see that an \nupward force acts on the float.\nFluids exert pressure in \nall directions.\nFigure 5.  Fluid exerted in all direction. From. https://www.slideshare.net/nosuhaila/chapter2-24683465\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "INDEPENDENCE OF SHAPE AND AREA.\nWater seeks its own level, \nindicating that fluid \npressure is independent of \narea and shape of its \ncontainer.\nAt any depth h below the surface of the \nwater in any column, the pressure P is the \nsame.  The shape and area are not factors.\nFigure 6. Independent of Shape and Area. From. https://www.slideshare.net/nosuhaila/chapter2-24683465\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "PROPERTIES OF FLUID PRESSURE\n• The forces exerted by a fluid on the walls of its \ncontainer are always perpendicular.\n• The fluid pressure is directly proportional to the \ndepth of the fluid and to its density.\n• At any particular depth, the fluid pressure is the \nsame in all directions.\n• Fluid pressure is independent of the shape or area of \nits container.\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "1. A diver is located 20 m below the surface of a \nlake (r = 1000 kg/m3). What is the pressure due \nto the water?\nh\nr = 1000 kg/m3\n∆P = ρg∆h =(1000 kg/m3)(9.8 m/s2)(20m)\n=196,000 Pa \nThe difference in pressure from \nthe top of the lake to the diver is:\n∆h = 20 m;  g = 9.8 m/s2\nSAMPLE\nFigure 7. Diver . From. https://www.slideshare.net/nosuhaila/chapter2-24683465\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "ATMOSPHERIC PRESSURE\nFor an open tank, we should include the pressure \noutside the tank pressing on the top of the surface of \nthe fluid, which is the atmospheric pressure Patm.\nPatm = 101.3 kPa = 760 torr = 760 mmHg\nPtotal = Patm+ ρgh\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "2. Compare the total pressure at the bottom of a \nswimming pool of depth 3.00m if it’s filled with \nfresh water and seawater.\nSOLUTION\nSAMPLE\nFor freshwater\nPtotal = Patm+ ρgh\n= 101.3 x103 Pa + (1x103 kg/m3)(9.81)(3.0m)\n= 130,730 Pa\nFor saltwater\nPtotal = Patm+ ρgh\n= 101.3 x103 Pa + (1.03x103 kg/m3)(9.81)(3.0m)\n= 131,612.9 Pa\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "CONVERSION OF PRESSURE UNITS\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "HYDRAULIC PRESSURE\nPressure in = Pressure out\nConsider a hydraulic jack filled with fluid everywhere.\nPascal’s principle states that “an external pressure \napplied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted uniformly \nthroughout the volume of the liquid.”\nFigure 8. Hydraulic Jack Filled with Fluid. \nFrom. https://www.chegg.com/homework-\nhelp/questions-and-answers/pascal-s-law-\nprinciple-transmission-fluid-pressure-states-\npressure-exerted-anywhere-confin-\nq3033100\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "The volume also are equal. Supposed pushing it with a \ndistance d1, on the left , then the amount of fluid \ndisplaced is the volume that that fluid that ends up at \nthe other side d2.\nIn other words, the same amount of fluid leaves on the left and \nenters on the right side.\nHYDRAULIC PRESSURE\nA1d1 = A2d2\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "3. A car that weighs 10.0 kN is placed on the 1.00 m radius \npiston of a hydraulic press. How much force should be exerted \nin 5.0 cm radius piston to lift the car?\nSAMPLE\nAnswer: 25 N\nSolution:\n𝐹!\"\n𝐴!\"\n= 𝐹#$%\n𝐴#$%\n𝐹!\" = 𝐴!\"𝐹#$%\n𝐴#$%\n𝐹!\" = 𝜋0.05𝑚& 10000𝑁\n𝜋1𝑚&\n= 25𝑁\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "REFERENCES\nSerway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nPhysics book by Gary B. Conrado and Maria A. Boncodin.\nSerway, R. A., & Vuille, C. (2015). College Physics 10th Edition. \nStamford, Connecticut: Cengage Learning. \nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "REFERENCES\nhttps://www.slideshare.net/nosuhaila/chapter2-24683465\nhttps://www.slideshare.net/reastment/pressure-in-fluids\nhttps://www.testo.com/en-IN/services/knowledgebase-pressure-\nphysical-principles\nhttps://www.chegg.com/homework-help/questions-and-\nanswers/pascal-s-law-principle-transmission-fluid-pressure-\nstates-pressure-exerted-anywhere-confin-q3033100\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "Archimedes’ Principle\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department │FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDefine Buoyant Force\n§\nState Archimedes’ Principle\n§\nSolve physical problems involving Archimedes’ Principle\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "Have you ever tried to push a ball down under water?\nIt is extremely difficult to do because of large upward force exerted by the water on the \nball.\nThis upward force is what we call Buoyant force.\nFigure 1. a) A swimmer pushes a beach ball under water. b) The forces on a beach ball–sized parcel of water. From. Physics for Scientists\nand Engineers with Modern Physics(pp 424) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "“The magnitude of the buoyant force on\nan object always equals the weight of the\nfluid displaced by the object.”\nFigure 2. Archimedes From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp 424) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019).\nAustralia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "An object that is completely or partially submerged in a fluid experiences \nan upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced.\nThe buoyant force is due to \nthe displaced fluid.  The \nblock material doesn’t \nmatter.\nFigure 3. Buoyant Force. From. https://www.slideshare.net/johnryanrizal/chapter15-\na?qid=783b9047-7f59-405c-8a1e-629327afd5ba&v=&b=&from_search=7\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "text": "CALCULATING BUOYANT FORCE\n𝑭𝑩= 𝝆𝒇𝒈𝑽\nBuoyant Force:\nThe buoyant force FB is due to the \ndifference of pressure DP between \nthe top and bottom surfaces of the \nsubmerged block.\nV is volume of fluid displaced.\n∆𝑃= 𝐹)\n𝐴= 𝑃& −𝑃*; 𝐹) = 𝐴𝑃& −𝑃*\n𝐹) = 𝐴𝑃& −𝑃* = 𝐴(𝜌+𝑔ℎ& −𝜌+𝑔ℎ*)\n𝐹) = (𝜌+𝑔)𝐴(ℎ& −ℎ*)\n𝑭𝑩= 𝝆𝒇𝒈𝑽\nFigure 4. Buoyant Force. Calculation From. \nhttps://www.slideshare.net/johnryanrizal/chapter15-a?qid=783b9047-7f59-405c-8a1e-\n629327afd5ba&v=&b=&from_search=7\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "text": "1. A 2-kg brass block is attached to a string and submerged underwater.  Find \nthe buoyant force and the tension in the rope\n.\nAll forces are balanced:\nmg\nFB = rgV\nT\nForce diagram\nFB + T = mg\nFB =  rwgVw\nFigure 5. Buoyant Force. Sample  From. https://www.slideshare.net/johnryanrizal/chapter15-\na?qid=783b9047-7f59-405c-8a1e-629327afd5ba&v=&b=&from_search=7\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "text": "When an object floats, partially submerged, the buoyant force \nexactly balances the weight of the object.\nFB = ρf gVf\nmx g = ρxVx g\nρf gVf = ρx Vx g\nrf Vf = rxVx\nFloating Objects:\nIf Vf is volume of displaced water \nVwd, the relative density of an \nobject x is given by:\nRelative Density:\nx\nwd\nr\nw\nx\nV\nV\nr\nr\nr\n=\n=\nFLOATING OBJECTS\nFigure 6.Floating Objects. From. https://www.slideshare.net/johnryanrizal/chapter15-\na?qid=783b9047-7f59-405c-8a1e-629327afd5ba&v=&b=&from_search=7\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "text": "2. A student floats in a salt lake with one-third of his body above the surface. If \nthe density of his body is 970 kg/m3, what is the density of the lake water?\n1/3\n2/3\nAssume the student’s volume is 3 m3.\nVs = 3 m3;  Vwd = 2 m3;  rs = 970 kg/m3\nFigure 7.Floating Objects. Sample From. \nhttps://www.slideshare.net/johnryanrizal/chap\nter15-a?qid=783b9047-7f59-405c-8a1e-\n629327afd5ba&v=&b=&from_search=7\nρw = 1460 kg/m3\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "text": "REFERENCES\nSerway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: \nCengage Learning.\nPhysics book by Gary B. Conrado and Maria A. Boncodin.\nSerway, R. A., & Vuille, C. (2015). College Physics 10th Edition. \nStamford, Connecticut: Cengage Learning. \nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "text": "REFERENCES\nhttps://www.slideshare.net/nosuhaila/chapter2-24683465\nhttps://www.slideshare.net/johnryanrizal/chapter15a?qid=783b9047-\n7f59-405c-8a1e-629327afd5ba&v=&b=&from_search=7\n", "image": "slides/m1-main/036.webp"}]}, {"id": "m1-dyn", "module": 1, "title": "Fluids & buoyancy", "variant": "dyn", "file": "Module 1 - Physics (Dyn).pdf", "sha256": "7f80b590b6ecc94fe42e692f97cd1e661e92cc9d8eaa7167bdbdb29d54e3c3c2", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "", "image": "slides/m1-dyn/012.webp"}]}, {"id": "m2-main", "module": 2, "title": "Temperature, heat & thermodynamics", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 2 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "885b6e4af066bd568c2ca06c775a77a9b1b65efe914e4e67aa9b2fe798285953", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "THERMAL PHYSICS\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department І FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "THERMAL EXPANSION\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department І FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "§\nExplain the basic concepts of thermal physics\n§\nDefine temperature and thermal expansion\n§\nSolve problems related to thermal expansion\nOBJECTIVES\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Temperature – it is the degree of hotness of an object \nand it can be measured by thermometer. \nUsually expressed in Celsius, Fahrenheit, or Kelvin\nScale.\nHeat – it is the energy which travels from high \ntemperature to low temperature in a matter. It is also \ncalled thermal or internal energy.\nDEFINITION OF TERMS\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Thermal Physics is the branch of physics that deals \nwith the study of temperature, heat energy and their \nrelation to matter.\nWhen objects are heated or cooled, the temperature \nchanges and some properties of the materials change. \nThis is called Thermometric Properties.\nDEFINITION OF TERMS\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "Mercury is a\ncommon liquid\nelement used in thermometer.\nIt freezes at -39°C and boils at\n357°C.\nFigure 1. A mercury thermometer before and after increasing its temperature. From. Physics for\nScientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp 570) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia:\nCengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "Conversion of Celsius to Fahrenheit:\nCONVERSION\nConversion of Farenheit to Celsius:\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "All matters expand when heated because of the increase in the \nvibration of the molecules.\nEXPANSION OF SOLIDS , LIQUIDS and GASES\nFigure 2  Thermal Expansion of Solids, Liquids and Gases. From. https://www.jagranjosh.com/general-knowledge/thermal-expansion-of-solids-liquids-and-gases-1458720400-1\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "A rod of a material has a length Lo at some initial \ntemperature T0. When the temperature changes by \nDT, the length changes by DL.\nFor moderate temperature changes, change in length \nis directly proportional to change in temperature.\nFigure 3. How the length of a rod changes with a change in temperature. (Length changes are exaggerated for clarity.)From. Sear's & Zemansky's University\nPhysics With Modern Physics(pp557) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\nLINEAR EXPANSION\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "The formula for linear expansion is given as \nfollows:\nD𝐿= α𝐿!D𝑇\nIf a rod has length Lo at temperature To, then its length \nL at a temperature T = To + DT is,\n𝐿= 𝐿! + D𝐿= 𝐿! + α𝐿!D𝑇\n𝑳= 𝑳𝒐(𝟏+ 𝜶D𝑻)\nWhere:   DL – change in length (m)\nLO– Initial length (m)\nL – final length (m)\nDT – change in temperature (C0 )\nα – coefficient of linear expansion (/C0)\nLINEAR EXPANSION\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "1. A Surveyors uses a steel measuring tape is exactly 50 \nm long at a temperature of 20°C. The markings on the \ntape are calibrated for this temperature. What is the \nlength of the tape when the temperature is 35°C? (αsteel\n= 11x 10-6 /C0)\nANSWER:  50.008m \nSAMPLE\nSOLUTION\n𝑳= 𝑳𝒐(𝟏+ 𝜶D𝑻)\n= 50m [1+ (11x 10-6 /C0) (35-20)C0]\n= 50.008m\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "When an object  \nundergoes thermal \nexpansion, any holes in \nthe object expand as \nwell. \nAREA EXPANSION\nFigure 4. Thermal Expansion in Holes. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics(pp557) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "The formula for area expansion is given as follows:\nD𝐴= 2α𝐴!D𝑇\nD𝐴= 𝛾𝐴!D𝑇\nIf a rod has length Lo at temperature To, then its length L at a \ntemperature T = To + DT is,\n𝐴= 𝐴! + D𝐴= 𝐴\" + 𝛾𝐴\"D𝑇= 𝐴\" + 2α𝐿\"D𝑇\n𝑨= 𝑨𝒐(𝟏+ 𝛾D𝑻)\nWhere:   DA – change in length (m2)\nAO– Initial length (m2)\nA – final length (m2)\nDT – change in temperature (C0 )\nα – coefficient of linear expansion (/C0)\nγ - coefficient of superficial  expansion (/C0)\nγ = 2α\nAREA EXPANSION (SUPERFICIAL EXPANSION)\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "CONCEPT CHECK\nSAMPLE\nWe cut a circular piece from the \nrectangular plate. Which ones of \nthe processes given below can help \nus in passing through the circular \npiece from the hole?\nI. Increasing the temperatures of rectangular plate and \ncircular piece\nII. Decreasing the temperature of the circular piece\nIII. Decreasing the temperatures of the rectangular plate and \ncircular piece\nFigure 4. Thermal Expansion in Holes. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics(pp557) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "Coefficients of Linear Expansion of Some Materials\nTable 1 . Coefficients of Linear Expansion of Some Materials . From. Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics(pp557) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis\nFord(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "Increasing temperature usually causes increases in \nvolume for both solid and liquid materials.\nD𝑉= β𝑉!D𝑇\nWhere:    DV – change in volume\nVO– Initial volume\nDT – change in temperature\nβ – coeffecient of volume expansion\nThe relationship between coefficient of linear \nexpansion and coefficient of volume expansion is:\nβ = 3α\nVOLUME EXPANSION\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "Coefficients of Volume  Expansion of Some Materials\nTable 2 . Coefficients of Volume Expansion of Some Materials . From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics(pp557) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016).\nEngland: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "2. A glass flask with volume 200cm3 is filled to the brim with \nmercury at 20 °C. How much mercury overflows when the \ntemperature of the system is raised to 100 °C? (The coefficient of \nlinear expansion of the glass is 0.40x10-5K-1. The coefficient of \nvolume expansion of mercury is 18x10-5K-1 )\nANSWER:  2.688cm3\nSAMPLE\nSOLUTION\n𝑉#!\"#$%#& −𝑉#'()** = 𝑉!\n1 + 𝛽∆𝑇$%&'(&) −1 + 𝛽∆𝑇*+,--\n(Mercury  expands faster than glass)\n= 200𝑐𝑚. E\nF\n1 + 18𝑥10/0/𝐶1 1001𝐶−201𝐶\n−1 + 3 ∗0.4𝑥10/0/𝐶1 1001𝐶−201𝐶\n= 2.688𝑐𝑚.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttps://www.physicstutorials.org/home/heat-temperature-and-\nthermal-expansion/thermal-expansion-and-contraction\nhttps://www.jagranjosh.com/general-knowledge/thermal-\nexpansion-of-solids-liquids-and-gases-1458720400-1\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "HEAT TRANSFER \n(THERMAL EQUILIBRIUM)\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department І FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDefine heat, specific heat and calorimetry\n§\nSolve problems related to calorimetry\n§\nState the Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics\n§\nExplain the First Law of Thermodynamics\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Energy transfer that takes \nplace solely because of \ntemperature difference is \ncalled heat flow or heat transfer\nand energy transferred in this \nway is called heat\nAccording to Joule, the temperature rise is directly proportional to the amount of work \ndone.\nHEAT\nHeat – it is the energy which travels from high temperature to \nlow temperature in a matter. It is also called thermal or \ninternal energy.\nFigure 1. The same temperature change of the same system may be accomplished by (a) doing work on it or (b) adding heat to it. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics(pp562) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "HEAT TRANSFER\nCalorimetry means measuring heat.\nCONDUCTION\n• Transfer of heat energy by molecular and electron collision \nwithin a substance (especially solid))\n• Materials that transfer heat through this manner is referred \nto as conductors\n• Solids are good conductors because their molecules are far \nfrom each other and liquids and gases are poor conductors \nsince their molecules are far from each other\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "HEAT TRANSFER\nCONVECTION\n•\nTransfer of heat energy in a gas or liquid by means \nof currents in the heated fluid. The fluid moves \ncarrying energy with it \nExample: hot air rising\nFigure 2. Heat Transfer. From.  \nhttps://www.simscale.com/docs/simwiki/heat-transfer-thermal-\nanalysis/what-is-heat-transfer/\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "HEAT TRANSFER\nRADIATION\n•\nRefers to the transfer of energy at \nthe speed of light through \nelectromagnetic waves\n•\nRadiant Energy- energy that is so \nradiated in a form of \nelectromagnetic waves\n•\nDark, rough surfaces absorb \nmore radiant energy and produce \nheat. Light, smooth materials \nreflect radiant energy and \nproduce less heat\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "Calorie – is defined as the amount of heat required to raise \nthe temperature of 1 gram of water from 14.5°C to 15.5°C.\nBritish Thermal Unit (BTU) – is the quantity of heat \nrequired to raise the temperature of 1 pound (weight) of \nwater 1°F \nQUANTITY OF HEAT\n1 cal = 4.186 J\n1 kcal = 1000 cal\n1 Btu = 778 ft lb = 252 cal\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "The quantity of heat Q required to increase the temperature of a \nmass m of a certain material from T1 to T2, is found to be \nproportional to the change in temperature and the mass of the \nmaterial.\n𝑸= 𝒎𝒄D𝑻\nWhere: Q – heat required (J)\nm – mass (kg)\nc – specific heat (J/kg C0 ) \nDT – change in temperature (C0)\nSpecific heat (c) is the amount of heat required to raise the \ntemperature by 1°C for the mass of 1kg.\nQUANTITY OF HEAT\nSpecific heat (c) of water:\nCwater = 4186 J/kg C0 = 1cal/g C0 = 1Btu/lb F0\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "If C is initially in thermal equilibrium with both A and B, then A and B are also in \nthermal equilibrium with each other.\nZEROTH LAW OF THERMODYNAMICS\nFigure 3. The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp553) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R.\nA. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "Two systems are in thermal equilibrium if and only if they have the same \ntemperature.\nZEROTH LAW OF THERMODYNAMICS\nFigure 4. Thermal Equilibrium . Fromhttps://www.assignmentpoint.com/science/physics/thermal-equilibrium.html\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "CONSERVATION OF ENERGY\nConservation of energy requires that the\namount of energy that leaves a body is equal the\namount of energy that enters another body in an \nisolated system.\n𝐐𝐠𝐚𝐢𝐧+ 𝐐𝐥𝐨𝐬𝐬= 𝟎\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "text": "1. A 80-kg man has ran a fever of 39°C instead of the normal body \ntemperature of 37°C. Assuming that the human body is mostly \nwater, how much heat is required to raise his temperature by that \namount? (Cwater = 4186 J/kg C0)\nSOLUTION\nSAMPLE\nANSWER: 669.76 kJ \nQ = mcDT\n= 80 kg (4186 J/kg C0) (2C0))\n= 669,760 J\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "text": "2. What is the final temperature when 625 grams of \nwater at 75°C loses 7.96 x 104 J?\nANSWER: 44.6°C\nSAMPLE\nQ = mcDT\n- 7.96 x 104 J = 0.625 kg (4186 J/kg C0) (TF -75C0)\nTF= 44.6 0C\nSOLUTION\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "text": "3. A 65 g piece of iron at 525°C is put into 635 grams of \nwater at  15°C. What is the final temperature of the \nwater and the iron? (ciron = 0.444 J/g-C)\nSAMPLE\nSOLUTION\nQ!\"#$ + Q%&'' = 0\nQ(\")*+ + Q#+&$ = 0\n𝑚𝑐∆𝑇,-./0+ 𝑚𝑐∆𝑇1023=0\n[0.635kg(4186J/kgC0)(Tf-15C0)]+[0.065kg(444J/kgC0)(Tf-525C0)]=0\nTf = 20.5 0C\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "text": "-\nA branch of science that deals with the properties of matter and \ntheir changes due to the effects of heat and work (Shavit and \nGutfinger, 2009).\n-\nComes from the Greek word which means the power of heat \n(Shavit and Gutfinger, 2009).\n-\nis the study of heat and work\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "text": "• Temperature – degree of hotness\n• Pressure – force per unit area\n• Density – mass per unit volume\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "text": "If C is initially is in thermal equilibrium with both A\nand B, then A and B are also in thermal equilibrium \nwith each other.\nFigure 5 The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp553) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/036.webp"}, {"page": 37, "text": "PHASE CHANGE\nresults when heat is absorbed or released without a change in temperature\nMelting/Freezing\nTo calculate for the amount of heat associated with a change in \nthe object’s phase, we use:\n𝑄= ±𝑚𝐿!\nheat\nmass\nlatent heat of \nfusion\nUse +Lf for melting and –Lf for freezing \namount of heat \nrequired to \nchange a \nsubstance from \nsolid to liquid\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/037.webp"}, {"page": 38, "text": "To calculate for the amount of heat associated with a change in \nthe object’s phase, we use:\n𝑄= ±𝑚𝐿\"\nPHASE CHANGE\nresults when heat is absorbed or released without a change in temperature\nMelting/Freezing\nheat\nmass\nlatent heat of \nvaporization\nUse +Lv for melting and –Lv for freezing \namount of heat \nrequired to \nchange a \nsubstance from \nliquid to gas\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/038.webp"}, {"page": 39, "text": "A plot of temperature versus energy added when 1.00 g of ice \ninitially at 30.0°C is converted to steam at 120.0°C.\nFigure 6 Plot of Temperature vs Energy. From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp 599) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett,\nJ. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/039.webp"}, {"page": 40, "text": "Heats of Fusion and Vaporization\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/040.webp"}, {"page": 41, "text": "Calculate the total energy needed for a 100 g \npiece of ice at -40°C to convert it into steam of \n110°C.\nSAMPLE PROBLEMS\nSOLUTION\n𝑄!\"!#$ = 𝑚𝑐%&'∆𝑇+ 𝑚𝐿( + 𝑚𝑐)#!'*∆𝑇+ 𝑚𝐿++𝑚𝑐+#,\"*∆𝑇\n= 0.1𝑘𝑔\n2090 U\n-\n./0+\n0𝐶1 −−40𝐶1\n+\n0.1𝑘𝑔\n3.35𝑥102 U\n-\n./ +\n0.1𝑘𝑔\n4186 U\n-\n./0+\n100𝐶1 −0𝐶1\n+\n0.1𝑘𝑔\n2.26𝑥103 U\n-\n./ +\n0.1𝑘𝑔\n2110 U\n-\n./0+\n110𝐶1 −100𝐶1\n= 𝟑. 𝟏𝟐𝒙𝟏𝟎𝟓𝑱\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/041.webp"}, {"page": 42, "text": "The change in the internal energy Δ𝑈of a closed system is \nequal to the energy added to the system through heat \n𝑄minus the work done by the system on its surroundings \n𝑊.\n𝚫𝑼= 𝑸−𝑾\nInternal Energy – the sum of the kinetic \nenergies of all its constituent particles plus \nthe sum of all the potential energies of the \ninteraction among the particles. \nClosed System – a system \nthat can exchange energy, \nbut not matter, with its \nsurroundings\nSign Conventions\n+Q – heat added\n-Q – heat loss\n+W – by the system\n-W – on the system\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/042.webp"}, {"page": 43, "text": "Let’s Try!\nTell whether the change in internal energy is due to heat \n(Q) or work (W):\n1. The soup in a pan sitting on an electric stove gets hot.\n2. Your hands are warmed when they’re placed near fire.\n3. The atmosphere warms a re-entering spacecraft.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/043.webp"}, {"page": 44, "text": "1. 3000 J of heat is added to a system and 2500 J of work is \ndone by the system. What is the change in its internal \nenergy?\nSolution: \nThe equation of the first law of thermodynamics\nΔU = Q-W\nΔU = 3000-2500\nΔU = 500 Joules\nInternal energy increases by 500 Joules.\nThe sign conventions :\nQ is positive if the heat added to the system\nW is positive if work is done by the system\nQ is negative if heat leaves the system\nW is negative if work is done on the system\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/044.webp"}, {"page": 45, "text": "2. Suppose there is a heat transfer of 40.00 J to a system, \nwhile the system does 10.00 J of work. Later, there is a \nheat transfer of 25.00 J out of the system while 4.00 J of \nwork is done on the system. What is the net change in \ninternal energy of the system?\nSolution:  \nThe net heat transfer is the heat transfer into the system minus the heat transfer out of the system, or\nQ = 40.00 J − 25.00 J = 15.00 J.\nSimilarly, the total work is the work done by the system minus the work done on the system, or\nW = 10.00 J − 4.00 J = 6.00 J.\nThus the change in internal energy is given by the first law of thermodynamics:\nΔU = Q −W = 15.00 J − 6.00 J = 9.00 J.\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/045.webp"}, {"page": 46, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttps://www.assignmentpoint.com/science/physics/thermal-\nequilibrium.html\nhttps://www.simscale.com/docs/simwiki/heat-transfer-thermal-\nanalysis/what-is-heat-transfer/\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m2-main/046.webp"}]}, {"id": "m2-dyn-alt", "module": 2, "title": "Temperature, heat & thermodynamics", "variant": "dyn-alt", "file": "Module 2 - Physics (Dyn) (1).pdf", "sha256": "ec46d7e5db02ecb74a70e01dd43d6b9a4f047b4c6df1334a43348e9dc6d5c436", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn-alt/010.webp"}]}, {"id": "m2-dyn", "module": 2, "title": "Temperature, heat & thermodynamics", "variant": "dyn", "file": "Module 2 - Physics (Dyn).pdf", "sha256": "1e2661eadeeb1b90920b007880eb6ef29e3280eb72713c729956d86cc109e3d7", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "", "image": "slides/m2-dyn/013.webp"}]}, {"id": "m3-main", "module": 3, "title": "Charge & Coulomb force", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 3 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "b2ea5068b36c5c7bdc02aa7b2cad915a92ea54e4e527a2fb01b81d4f4cd6ad13", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "ELECTRIC CHARGE\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department │FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "CHARGES\nCollege Physics 2\nMPS Department │FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nExplain the basic concepts of charge and conservation of charges\n§\nDifferentiate the types of charging\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "The word “electric” is derived from the Greek word \nelektron, meaning amber.\nThey discovered that when they rubbed amber with \nwool, the amber could attract other objects.\nElectricity is an invisible force in nature consisting of \ntwo electrical charges, the proton and electron\nELECTRIC CHARGE\nTrains, electronic gadgets, heating and cooling appliances, and many more, even our brain uses \nelectricity to work.\nFigure 1. Amber. \nFrom.https://phys.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/7959/\nCNX_UPhysics_22_01_Amber.jpg?revision=1\nFigure 2. Wool. From. https://images.pexels.com/photos/317333/pexels-\nphoto-317333.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&dpr=2&w=500\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Electrical charge is a property of matter that causes it to produce and \nexperience electrical and magnetic effect\nIt can be represented by the symbol (Q) with a unit called Coulomb that \nis represented by C.\nThe unit of electrical charge is named after \nCharles Augustin De Coulomb.\nThe charge of an electron or proton:\n𝒆= ±𝟏. 𝟔𝟎𝟐𝒙𝟏𝟎!𝟏𝟗𝑪\nELECTRIC CHARGE\nCoulomb found out that each electric point \ncharge exerts a mechanical force on the other.\nFigure 3. Portrait of Charles Augustin de Coulomb.[image] Retrieve \nfrom http://en.citizendium.org/wiki/Charles-Augustin_de_Coulomb\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "Plastic rods and fur are particularly good for demonstrating electrostatics, \nthe interactions between electric charges that are at rest.\nBenjamin Franklin suggested to calling the two kinds of charges which are \npositive and negative charges.\nThe key concept is: Two positive charges or two negative charges repel \neach other. A positive and a negative charge attract each other.\nELECTRIC CHARGE\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "When a material has an excess number of electrons, it has a negative \nelectrical charge but when a material has a deficiency of electrons, \nit has a positive electrical charge.\nELECTRIC CHARGE\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "(1) Quantization of Charge : Electric charge can have only discrete values, \nthat is, charge is quantized. A charge q must be an integral multiple of this \nbasic unit. That is,\nQ = ± ne\nwhere n = 1, 2, … and e= ± 1.602x10-19C \nA typical charge of a rubbed body is 10-8 C.\nPROPERTIES OF CHARGE\nA body is having a charge of +0.32 C. How many electrons have been \nremoved from it ?\nSOLUTION\nSince charge Q=+0.32 C, there electrons are removed from the body.\nLet n be the no. of electrons\n𝒏= 𝟎. 𝟑𝟐𝑪\n𝟏𝒆\n𝟏. 𝟔𝟎𝟐𝒙𝟏𝟎!𝟏𝟗𝑪= 𝟐𝒙𝟏𝟎𝟏𝟖𝒆\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "(2) Charge is always associated with mass : A charge cannot exist without \nmass, though a mass can exist without charge. The particles such \nas photon or neutrino have no (rest) mass. Hence, these particles can never \nhave a charge.\nThe mass of a body (slightly) increases when it acquires a negative charge (by \ngaining some electrons). On the other hand, when a body acquires a positive \ncharge (by losing some electrons), its mass (slightly) decreases.\nPROPERTIES OF CHARGE\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "(2) Conservation of charge : Electric charge is always conserved in an \nisolated system. One object gains some amount of negative charge while the \nother gains an equal amount of positive charge.\n(3) Invariance of charge : Numerical value of a charge is independent of the \nframe of reference. It means the value of charge on a body remains the same, \nwhether it is stationary, or moving with a constant velocity or accelerating.\nPROPERTIES OF CHARGE\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "ELECTRIC CHARGE\nExperiments in Electrostatics\nFigure 4. Experiments in Electrostatics. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 688) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "CONDUCTION – charging that involves contact\n• Conductors – materials that permit electric charge to move easily from \none region of the material to another.\n• Insulators – material that do not easily permit the electric charge to \nflow. \n• Semiconductor – is a material that has an intermediate property of \nconductor and insulator.\nCHARGING PROCESS\nFigure 5. Charging Process From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics(pp 692) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. &\nA. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "INDUCTION – Charging process that does not involve \ncontact\nCHARGING PROCESS\nFigure 6. Charging a Metal by Induction. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics(pp 692) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "FRICTION objects are rubbed with each other and \ncharges transfer\nCHARGING PROCESS\nAfter you electrify a comb by \nrunning it through your hair, \nyou can pick up uncharged \nbits of paper with the comb. \nThis interaction is called an \ninduced – charged effect.\nThe charges within the molecules of an insulating material can shift \nslightly. This is called Polarization ( atomic level of separation of charges \nin insulators\nFigure 7. The charges within the molecules of an insulating material can shift slightly. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With\nModern Physics(pp 693) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttps://www.dronstudy.com/book/chapter-notes-electrostatics-class-\n12/\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "https://phys.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/7959/CNX_UPhysics_22_0\n1_Amber.jpg?revision=1\nhttps://images.pexels.com/photos/317333/pexels-photo-\n317333.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&dpr=2&w=500\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "ELECTRIC FORCE \n(Coulomb’s Law)\nCOLLEGE Physics 2\nMPS Department │FEU Institute of Technology\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nExplain the basic concepts of electrostatic forces\n§\nBe able to solve problems related to Coulomb’s Law\n§\nSolve problems related to Coulomb’s Law using superposition principle\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "“The magnitude of the electric force between two point\ncharges is directly proportional to the product of the \ncharges and inversely proportional to the square of the \ndistance between them”\n𝐹$ =\n1\n4πƐ%\n𝑞&𝑞'\n𝑟'\n= 𝑘$\n𝑞&𝑞'\n𝑟'\nWhere: F – Force in Newton\nƐ0 – Permittivity of free space\nq1 and q2 – Charges in Coulomb (C)\nr – distance between the charges in meters (m)\nke – force constant (Nm2/C2)\nke =\n!\n\"#Ɛ! = 8.99 x 10% &'\"\n(\"\nCOULOMB’S LAW\nFigure 8. Tyler (2014), Coulomb’s Law (image). Retrieved from \nhttp://www.wirebiters.com/electrostatics-coulombs-law/\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "Vector form of electric force between \ncharges q1 and q2:⃑\n𝐹!\" = 𝑘#\n𝑞!𝑞\"\n𝑟!\"\"̂ 𝑟!\"\nCOULOMB’S LAW\n(a) When the charges are of the \nsame sign, the force is repulsive. \n(b) When the charges are of \nopposite signs, the force is \nattractive\nFigure 8. Tyler (2014), Coulomb’s Law (image). Retrieved from \nhttp://www.wirebiters.com/electrostatics-coulombs-law/\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "1. The Hydrogen Atom: The electron and proton of a hydrogen \natom are separated by a distance of approximately 5.3 × 10-11 m. \nFind the magnitude of the electric force and gravitational between \nthe two particles.\nSAMPLE\nSOLUTION\n𝐹) = 𝑘)\n*#*\"\n+\"\n= 8.99𝑥10%\n2\n&,'\"\n+\"\n!../01!/$#%(\n\"\n2.31!/$##' \"\n𝐹)= 8.2 𝑥1045𝑁\n𝐹6 = 𝐺\n'&''\n+\"\n= 6.67𝑥104!!\n2\n&'\"\n76\"\n%.!!1!/(#76\n!..81!/$\")76\n2.31!/$##' \"\n= 3.6𝑥104\"8𝑁\nPARTICLE\nCHARGE (C)\nMASS (kg)\nElectron (e)\n-1.602x10-19\n9.1094x10-31\nProton (p)\n+1.602x10-19\n1.67262x10-27\nNeutron\n0\n1.67262x10-27\nGravitational force is very small compared to the electric force between the electron and proton.\nTable 1. Retrieved from https://profiles.uonbi.ac.ke/nyangondat/files/lesson_1_1_electrostatics_and_coulombs_law.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "2. Two point charges, q1 = 25nC and q2 = -75nC, are \nseparated by a distance of 3.0 cm. Find the magnitude and \ndirection of (a) the electric force that q1 exerts on q2 and (b) \nthe electric force that q2 exerts on q1.\nSAMPLE\n(a)     𝐹%& = 𝑘'!'\"\n(\"\n= 8.99𝑥10)\n3\n*+,\"\n(\"\n&-.%/#$0\n!1--.%/#$0\n2.%/#\", \"\n(b) 𝐹%& = 𝐹&% = 0.019𝑁\nSOLUTION\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "RECALL:\nVector form of electric force between \ncharges q1 and q2:⃑\n𝐹%& = 𝑘3\n𝑞%𝑞&\n𝑟%&&̂ 𝑟%&\nSUPERPOSITION PRINCIPLE\nThe net force on q1 due to other charges:\n𝑭𝟏= 𝑭𝟐𝟏+𝑭𝟑𝟏+𝑭𝟒𝟏\nWhere⃑\n𝐹!\" is the force on q1 due to q2⃑\n𝐹#\" is the force on q1 due to q3⃑\n𝐹$\" is the force on q1 due to q4\nFigure 9.Forces acting on q1 due to q2, q3, and q4 (image). Retrieved from \nhttps://www.dronstudy.com/book/chapter-notes-electrostatics-class-12/\nConsider more than two charges, to find the net electric force on a charge, \nwe will be using superposition principle, that is, addition of vectors. \n", "image": "slides/m3-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "1. Two point charges are located on the positive x-axis of a \ncoordinate system. Charge q1 = 1.0 nC is 2.0 cm from the \norigin and charge q2 = -3.0 nC is 4.0 cm from the origin. \nWhat is the total electric force exerted on a charge q3 = 5.0 \nnC located at the origin? \nSAMPLE\nFigure 10. Two Point Charges. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics(pp 698) by \nYoung, H. D., Freedman, R. A.  &  A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "Let ⃑𝐹\" 𝑏𝑒𝑡ℎ𝑒𝑓𝑜𝑟𝑐𝑒𝑜𝑛𝑞# 𝑑𝑢𝑒𝑡𝑜𝑞\" and ⃑𝐹! 𝑏𝑒𝑡ℎ𝑒𝑓𝑜𝑟𝑐𝑒𝑜𝑛𝑞# 𝑑𝑢𝑒𝑡𝑜𝑞!.⃑\n𝐹T3U =⃑𝐹%+⃑𝐹&⃑\n𝐹T3U\n= −8.99𝑥10)\n3\n𝑁= 𝑚&\n𝑟&\n5𝑥10!)𝐶\n1𝑥10!)𝐶\n2𝑥10!&𝑚&̂\n𝚤\n+ 8.99𝑥10)\n3\n𝑁= 𝑚&\n𝑟&\n5𝑥10!)𝐶\n−3𝑥10!)𝐶\n4𝑥10!&𝑚&̂\n𝚤⃑\n𝐹T3U = −28x10!%V𝑁̂𝚤\nSAMPLE\nANSWER: Fnet = 28µN going to the left\nFigure 10. Two Point Charges. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern \nPhysics(pp 698) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A.  &  A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "EXERCISE\n2. Four-point charges are located at the corners of a rectangle, as \nshown in figure. Find the net force acting on the charge q1\nFigure 11. Four-point charges at the corners of a rectangle (image). Retrieved from https://www.dronstudy.com/book/chapter-\nnotes-electrostatics-class-12/\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "EXERCISE\n2. Four point charges are located at the corners of a rectangle, as \nshown in figure. Find the net force acting on the charge q1\nFigure 12. Four-point charges at the corners of a \nrectangle (image). Retrieved from \nhttps://www.dronstudy.com/book/chapter-notes-\nelectrostatics-class-12/\nSOLUTION\nThe force ⃑𝐹0! is repulsive, while ⃑𝐹3! and ⃑𝐹\"!\nare attractive.\nFind the magnitude:\n𝐹+, = 𝑘𝑞,𝑞+\n𝑟,++ = 9𝑥10-\n*\n𝑁𝑚+\n𝐶+\n8𝑥10.-𝐶\n4𝑥10.-𝐶\n3𝑥10.+ +\n= 32𝑥10./𝑁\n𝐹0, = 𝑘𝑞,𝑞0\n𝑟,0+ = 9𝑥10-\n*\n𝑁𝑚+\n𝐶+\n8𝑥10.-𝐶\n8𝑥10.-𝐶\n5𝑥10.+ +\n= 23𝑥10./𝑁\n𝐹1, = 𝑘𝑞,𝑞+\n𝑟,++ = 9𝑥10-\n*\n𝑁𝑚+\n𝐶+\n8𝑥10.-𝐶\n12𝑥10.-𝐶\n4𝑥10.+ +\n= 54𝑥10./𝑁\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "EXERCISE\nFigure 12. Four-point charges at the corners of a \nrectangle (image). Retrieved from \nhttps://www.dronstudy.com/book/chapter-notes-\nelectrostatics-class-12/\nSOLUTION\nWriting in i-j notations:⃑\n𝐹= 𝐹𝑐𝑜𝑠𝜃̂𝚤+ 𝐹𝑠𝑖𝑛𝜃̂𝚥⃑\n𝐹+, = 32𝑥10./𝑁cos −902̂ 𝚤+ 32𝑥10./𝑁𝑠𝑖𝑛−902̂ 𝚥⃑\n𝐹+, = 32𝑥10./𝑁̂ 𝚥⃑\n𝐹0, = −23𝑥10./𝑁4\n5̂ 𝚤+ 23𝑥10./𝑁3\n5̂ 𝚥⃑\n𝐹0, = −18.4𝑥10./𝑁̂ 𝚤+ 13.8𝑥10./𝑁̂ 𝚥⃑\n𝐹1, = 54𝑥10./𝑁cos 1802 + 54𝑥10./𝑁𝑠𝑖𝑛1802⃑\n𝐹1, = −54𝑥10./𝑁̂ 𝚤⃑\n𝐹, =⃑𝐹+,+⃑𝐹0,+⃑𝐹1,⃑\n𝐹, = −72.4𝑁̂𝚤−18.2𝑁𝑗𝑥10./\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and Engineers with \nModern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics \nWith Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttp://www.wirebiters.com/electrostatics-coulombs-law/\nhttps://profiles.uonbi.ac.ke/nyangondat/files/lesson_1_1_electrostatics_and_coul\nombs_law.pdf\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "https://www.dronstudy.com/book/chapter-notes-electrostatics-class-12/\nhttps://images.pexels.com/photos/317333/pexels-photo-\n317333.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&dpr=2&w=500\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m3-main/030.webp"}]}, {"id": "m3-dyn", "module": 3, "title": "Charge & Coulomb force", "variant": "dyn", "file": "Module 3 - Physics (Dyn).pdf", "sha256": "72dd4147769f671ca457d108fce173141b5be61115b2cc7436dc2de8ba06fcf3", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "", "image": "slides/m3-dyn/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "", "image": "slides/m3-dyn/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "", "image": "slides/m3-dyn/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "", "image": "slides/m3-dyn/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "", "image": "slides/m3-dyn/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "", "image": "slides/m3-dyn/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "", "image": "slides/m3-dyn/007.webp"}]}, {"id": "m4-main", "module": 4, "title": "Electric fields", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 4 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "68dae9ff3171e8af3ce14600c35034d30a62edd852b5314113d76a7f77e8bfdc", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Electric Field\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Electric Field \nPrinciples\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "OBJECTIVE\n§\nState general properties of electric field.\n§\nInterpret electric field using electric field lines.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Electric field is defined as the area where electrical \nforce can be experienced.\nA charged body creates an electric field in the space \naround it.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Suppose we have two charges A and B. B has a charge q0 and \nexperiences a force F0 due to A. A, on the other hand, also \nexperiences a force F0 in the opposite direction. \nFigure 1. a) A charged body creates an electric field in the space around it. . From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics (pp 699) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "Figure 1. b) A charged body creates an electric field in the space around it. . From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 699) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\nCharge A modifies the properties of space around it.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "Figure 1. c) A charged body creates an electric field in the space around it. . From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics (pp 699) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "•\nIt’s actually the electric field that exerts the force on the\ncharge.\n•\nThe charged particles DO NOT require the presence of\nanother particle to produce an electric field. They\nproduce the electric field by themselves.\nP\nF0\nIt is actually the electric field that \nexerts a force on the particles.\nFigure 1. c) A charged body creates an electric field in the space around it. . From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics (pp 699) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "The particles are able to create an electric field as a result\nof the charge they carry. This somehow modifies the\nproperties of the space around them.\nFigure 2. Electric Field Lines. Retrieved from  https://brilliant.org/wiki/electric-field-lines/\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "Electric field is \ndefined as the force \nper unit charge\nexperienced by a \nstationary point \ncharge in a given\nlocation in a field.\nIn equation:\n𝑬=⃑𝐹\n𝑞\nwhere:\nE – electric field in N/C\nF – force in N \nq – is the charge in C\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "The strength of an electric field E \nat any point may be defined as the \nelectric force F exerted per unit \npositive electric charge q at that \npoint, or simply E = F/q.\nUsing Coulomb’s Law, the \nmagnitude of the electric field at \nany given point around a charge \ncan be determined.\n! = !\n! =\n!!!!!\n!!\n!\n= !\"\n!!!\nWhere \nE = electric field (N/C)\nF = electric force (N)\nq = charge (C) \nr = distance between charges (m)\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "1. The positive test charge in the figure shown has \na magnitude of  3.0x10-8C and experiences a \nforce of 6.0x10-8N.\nFind the force per coulomb that the test charge \nexperiences.\nPredict the force that a charge of +12x10-8C would \nexperience if it replaced the test charge.\nSolution :\n(a) \n(b) \nC\nN\n0.2\nC\n10\n0.3\nN\n10\n0.6\n8\n8\n=\n´\n´\n=\n-\n-\noq\nF\n(\n)(\n)\nN\n10\n24\nC\n10\n0.\n12\nC\nN\n0.2\n8\n8\n-\n-\n´\n=\n´\n=\nF\nFigure 3. Electric Field. Retrieved from http://physicsskills.pbworks.com/f/E%2BForces%2Band%2BE%2BFields.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "The electric field points \naway from a positive \ncharge and toward a \nnegative charge and the \nelectric field \nstrength/magnitude \ndecreases with distance.\nFigure 4. Equivalent representations  of the electric field  Retrieved from https://courses.lumenlearning.com/physics/chapter/18-5-\nelectric-field-lines-multiple-charges/\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "Michael Faraday (1791-1867) first introduced the concept of field lines. He \ncalled them “lines of force” but the term “field lines” is preferable.\nElectric field lines for three different charge distributions. In general the magnitude of 𝐸is \ndifferent at different points along a given field. \nFigure 5. Electric field lines \nfor three different charge \ndistributions. From. Sear's & \nZemansky's University Physics \nWith Modern Physics (pp 699) \nby Young, H. D., Freedman, R. \nA.  &  A. Lewis Ford(2016). \nEngland: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "An electric field line is an imaginary line or \ncurve drawn through a region of empty space so \nthat its tangent at any point is in the direction of \nthe electric field vector at that point. \n", "image": "slides/m4-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "The combination of 2 equal charges with \nopposite signs is called electric dipole.\nThe electric field lines are curved and are \ndirected from the positive charge to the negative \ncharge.\nThe direction of the electric field at any point is \ntangent to the field of line at any point (as shown \nby the arrow at arbitrary point P) \nThe electric field lines due to two EQUAL \ncharges of OPPOSITE signs, a combination \nknown as ELECTRIC DIPOLE.\nFigure 6. Electric Field Lines due to two equal charges  of opposite signs.Retrieved from \nhttps://courses.lumenlearning.com/physics/chapter/18-5-electric-field-lines-multiple-charges/\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "Electric field lines for 2 EQUAL POSITIVE charges\nElectric field lines for 2 EQUAL NEGATIVE charges\nFigure 7 Electric Field Lines for 2  Equal Positive (Negative) charges. Retrieved from https://courses.lumenlearning.com/physics/chapter/18-5-electric-field-lines-multiple-charges/\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "1.\nElectric field lines indicate the direction of the electric field; the field points \nin the  direction tangent to the field line at any point\n2.\nThe lines are drawn so that the magnitude of the electric field E is \nproportional to  the number of lines crossing unit area perpendicular to the \nlines. The closer  together the lines, the stronger the field.\n3.\nElectric field lines start on positive charges and end on negative charges; \nand the  number starting or ending is proportional to the magnitude of the\ncharge.\nNote: Field lines do not cross because it would not make sense for the electric field to have 2 directions at the \nsame point.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "The number of lines leaving a\npositive charge or entering a\nnegative charge is proportional\nto the magnitude of the charge.\nFigure 8. Electric Field Lines leaving a positive charge  or entering a negative charge.. Retrieved from \nhttps://courses.lumenlearning.com/physics/chapter/18-5-electric-field-lines-multiple-charges/\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "There are three things wrong with part (a) \nof  the drawing.  What are they?\nAnswer: \n1) Field lines can never cross.\n2) The number of electric field lines that \nend on the negative charges.\n3) Spacing of electric field lines.\nFigure 9. Conceptual Sample . Retrieved from https://courses.lumenlearning.com/physics/chapter/18-5-electric-\nfield-lines-multiple-charges/\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and Engineers with \nModern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics \nWith Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttps://brilliant.org/wiki/electric-field-lines/\nhttp://physicsskills.pbworks.com/f/E%2BForces%2Band%2BE%2BFields.pdf\nhttps://courses.lumenlearning.com/physics/chapter/18-5-electric-field-lines-\nmultiple-charges/\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Motion of Point Charges in  \nElectric Field\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDescribe the motion of charges in electric field\n§\nSolve problems involving  discrete and continuous charge distribution. \n", "image": "slides/m4-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "An electron moves in a uniform electric field\n! = !\n!!\n! = !\"!!!!\n! = −!!\n! = −!\"!\n! = !\"!\n−!\" = !\"!\n! = −!\"\n!!\n!\nFigure 10. Electron moves in a Uniform Electric Field. From. Essential University Physics (pp 350) by Wolfson R.(2012). \nAddison-Wesley, Pearson\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "A positive charge moves in a uniform electric \nfield\n! = !\n!!\n! = !\"!!!!\n! = !!!\n! = !\"!\n!! = !\"!\n! = !!\n! !\n!\nFigure 11. A positive point charge in a uniform electric field. From. Physics\nfor Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp 711) by Serway, R. A., &\nJewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "MAGNITUDE\nIf we place a small test charge q0 at the field point P, at a distance r from the source point, the \nmagnitude is given by Coulomb’s Law: \n𝐹=\n1\n4πƐ!\n𝑞𝑞!\n𝑟\"\n1\n𝑞!\n𝐸=\n1\n4πƐ!\n𝑞\n𝑟\"\n= 𝑘\n𝑞\n𝑟\"\nELECTRIC FIELD OF A POINT CHARGE\nWhere:\nE – Electric Field in (N/C)\nk – Coulomb constant\nk ≈9.0 x 109 Nm2/C2\nq – Charge in Coulomb (C)\nr – distance from the charge to point P in     \nmeters (m)\nF – Electrical Force in newton (N)\nq0 – test charge in Coulomb (C)\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "DIRECTION\n(a) If q is positive, the force on the test charge is directed away from q.\n(b) For the positive source charge, the electric field at P points radially outward from q. \nELECTRIC FIELD OF A POINT CHARGE\nFigure 12. a) Force on a Test Charge. b) Electric Field of a Test Charge From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp\n700) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "DIRECTION\n(c) If q is negative, the force on the test charge is directed towards  q.\n(d) For the negative source charge, the electric field at P points radially inward from q. \nELECTRIC FIELD OF A POINT CHARGE\nFigure 12. c) Force on a Test Charge. d) Electric Field of a Test Charge From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp\n700) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "1. What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point 2.0 m from a point charge \nq = 4.0 nC?\nSOLUTION\n𝐸= 𝑘\n𝑞\n𝑟!\n= 9.0𝑥10\"\n+\n𝑁𝑚! 𝐶! #$%&!\"'\n!( #\n= 9.0𝑁/𝐶\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "2. What is the magnitude of a point charge that would create an electric field of 5N/C at \npoints 150 cm away?\nSOLUTION\n𝐸= 𝑘\n𝑞\n𝑟\"\nq =\n)*#\n+\nq = 1.25 x 10#$ C\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "text": "If many charges are present in a given region, each charge contributes to the electric field at a \nknown point. The Net electric field is the vector sum of all the fields of the charges that is,\n𝐸# = 𝐸$ + 𝐸\" + ⋯+ 𝐸%\nELECTRIC FIELD OF POINT CHARGES\nFigure 13. The total electric field. From. https://www.miniphysics.com/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2010/11/electric-field-between-point-charges.png\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "text": "3. Two charges of -5.00 nC and -3.00 nC are 1.5 m apart. Compute for the electric field halfway \nbetween them.\nSOLUTION\nLet E1 be the magnitude of electric field due to \nq1 = -5.00nC\nE2 be the magnitude of electric field due    \nto q2=-3.00nC\n𝐸! = 𝑘𝑞!\n𝑟!\"\n𝐸! = 9.0𝑥10#\n⁄\n𝑁𝑚\" 𝐶\" $%&!'!\"(\n'.*%+#\n= 80𝑁/𝐶, left\n𝐸\" = 𝑘𝑞\"\n𝑟\"\"\n𝐸\" = 9.0𝑥10#\n⁄\n𝑁𝑚\" 𝐶\" $,&!'!\"(\n'.*%+#\n= 48𝑁/𝐶, right\n𝐸= −80 𝑁\n𝐶+ 48 𝑁\n𝐶= −32 𝑁/𝐶\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: \nCengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttps://www.miniphysics.com/wp \ncontent/uploads/2010/11/electric-field-between-point-\ncharges.png\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m4-main/033.webp"}]}, {"id": "m4-dyn", "module": 4, "title": "Electric fields", "variant": "dyn", "file": "Module 4 - Physics (Dyn).pdf", "sha256": "eabd8d9a5fe8008e0471e6baf0f87a48e96a3a8774fbcb71510f9510481e9f3c", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "", "image": "slides/m4-dyn/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "", "image": "slides/m4-dyn/002.webp"}]}, {"id": "m5-main", "module": 5, "title": "Electric potential & energy", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 5 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "c56ec5bcf3ea366342d262861f8b63f129b1e984752eff418982a483c641c869", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Electric Potential and \nPotential Difference\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Work and Electric \nPotential Energy\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDefine electric potential and electric potential energy\n§\nCalculate the work done in moving a charge in a region of constant electric field.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Electric potential energy is \nthe energy that charged \nparticles such as electrons \nand protons have because \nof its own electric charge \nand its relative position to \nother charged particles.\nFigure 1. Electric potential energy. From.https://cdn.sparkfun.com/r/600-600/assets/2/3/5/1/e/51a4f1a7ce395f4125000000.png\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Work is a scalar quantity equal to the product of the\ndisplacement x and the component of the force Fx in the\ndirection of the displacement.\nWork = Fx x\nWork = (F cos q) x\nx\nFigure 2. Work done at a certain angle. From. https://qph.fs.quoracdn.net/main-qimg-\n5dd99f3fd1d9aff67e96375dc46f61ff\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "Potential Energy:  Ability to do work by virtue of position or \ncondition.  \nU\nmgh\n=\nKinetic Energy:  Ability to do work by virtue of motion.  (Mass \nwith velocity)\n2\n1\n2\nK\nmv\n=\nThe Work-Energy Theorem:  The work done by a resultant \nforce is equal to the change in kinetic energy that it produces.\nWork = ½ mvf\n2 - ½ mvo\n2\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "Potential Energy and Work\n𝑃𝐸= 𝑚𝑔𝑦\nΔ𝑃𝐸!→# = 𝑚𝑔𝑦# −𝑚𝑔𝑦!\n= 𝑚𝑔Δ𝑦= 𝐹$Δ𝑦\n𝑊!→# = 𝐹%(𝑦# −𝑦!)\n= 𝐹$Δ𝑦\nThe amount of work done is equal to the \nchange in potential energy.\n𝑊=⃑𝐹*⃑𝑑= 𝐹𝑑𝑐𝑜𝑠𝜃\nFigure 3. Work and potential energy. From. \nhttps://kaiserscience.files.wordpress.com/2015/09/work-energy-and-\npower-30-638.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "§ The field exerts a downward \nforce on the positive test \ncharge.\n§ The charge moves from point \na to b.\n§ Work done by the field is \npositive.\n𝑊!→# = 𝑞$𝐸𝑦# −𝑦! = 𝑞$𝐸𝑑\nFigure 4. Work done by the electric force. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics (pp 755) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL ENERGY AND WORK (done by \ne-field)\n§ Since work done by the field \nas the charge moves from \npoint a to b is equal to the \nchange in potential energy \nfrom a to b, \n𝜟𝑼= 𝒒𝟎𝑬𝒚𝒃−𝒒𝟎𝑬𝒚𝒂\n= 𝒒𝟎𝑬−𝒅= −𝑾𝒂→𝒃\n𝑊%→& = −Δ𝑈\nFigure 4. Work done by the electric force. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics (pp 755) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL ENERGY AND WORK (done by \ne-field)\n𝑊!→# = −Δ𝑈\n§ When work done by the \nfield is positive, potential \nenergy decreases.\n§ When work done by the \nfield is negative, potential \nenergy increases.\nFigure 5. Motion of positive charge. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 756) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL ENERGY AND WORK (done \nby e-field)\nFigure 6. Motion of negative charge. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 756) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL ENERGY AND WORK (done by \ne-field)\nConceptual Question\nA proton moves from point i to point f, in the direction of \na uniform electric field. During this displacement:\na.\nthe work done by the field is positive and the potential energy of the electron-field \nsystem increases\nb.\nthe work done by the field is negative and the potential energy of the electron-\nfield system increases\nc.\nthe work done by the field is positive and the potential energy of the electron-field \nsystem decreases\nd.\nthe work done by the field is negative and the potential energy of the electron-\nfield system decreases\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL ENERGY AND WORK (done by \ne-field)\nConceptual Question\nA proton moves from point i to point f, in the direction of \na uniform electric field. During this displacement:\na.\nthe work done by the field is positive and the potential energy of the electron-field \nsystem increases\nb.\nthe work done by the field is negative and the potential energy of the electron-\nfield system increases\nc.\nthe work done by the field is positive and the potential energy of the electron-field \nsystem decreases\nd.\nthe work done by the field is negative and the potential energy of the electron-\nfield system decreases\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "Depends only on the endpoints\nForce on the test charge  due to the charge q\nThe work done on  by  in moving from a to b\nFigure 7. Electric potential energy of two point charges. From. Sear's\n& Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 756) by\nYoung, H. D., Freedman, R. A.\n&\nA. Lewis Ford(2016). England:\nPearson.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "Note on Potential Energy\n15\nIf q and are infinitely apart \n", "image": "slides/m5-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "Work and Potential Energy\nu Work that is done by the electric force when the particle\nmoves from a - b\nu Work that must be done by an external force to move the\nparticle from a – b against the electric force\n16\nU\nU\nU\nW\nW\na\nb\nelectric\nexternal\nD\n=\n-\n=\n-\n=\nU\nU\nU\nW\nb\na\nelectric\nD\n-\n=\n-\n=\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "Find the work that must be done by an external force to bring a third \npoint charge q3=+e from infinity to x=2a.\ninitial\nfinal\nexternal\nU\nU\nW\n-\n=\na\nke\nW\na\ne\na\ne\nke\nU\nU\nW\nq\nx\na\nx\nq\n2\n0\n2\n2\n2\n3\n3\n+\n=\n-\n÷\nø\nö\nç\nè\næ\n+\n-\n=\n-\n=\n¥\n=\n=\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "https://cdn.sparkfun.com/r/600-\n600/assets/2/3/5/1/e/51a4f1a7ce395f4125000000.png\nhttps://qph.fs.quoracdn.net/main-qimg\n7cd2ff936c83c099652dd85e2c2d77bb\nhttps://kaiserscience.files.wordpress.com/2015/09/work-energy-\nand-power-30-638.jpg\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "Electric Potential and \nElectric Potential Difference\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDefine electric potential and electric potential difference\n§\nCalculate electric potential of a collection of charges\n§\nUse equipotential surfaces to visualize how the electric potential varies in space\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "The electric potential energy of a test charge \nq0 in a field produced by charge q is given by\n𝑼= 𝒌𝒒𝟎𝒒\n𝒓\nwhere:\n𝑼is the electric potential energy in joules (J)\n𝒌=\n𝟏\n𝟒𝝅𝝐𝟎= 𝟗. 𝟎𝐱𝟏𝟎𝟗Nm2/C2\nr is the distance from q0 to q (from test charge \nto source) in m\nFor a system of point charges, the \nelectric potential energy is given \nby:\n𝑼= 𝒌𝒒𝟏𝒒𝟐\n𝒓𝟏𝟐\n+ 𝒒𝟐𝒒𝟑\n𝒓𝟐𝟑\n+ 𝒒𝟏𝒒𝟑\n𝒓𝟏𝟑\n+ ⋯\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "Electric Potential Energy for several point charges\nu Total electric field is the vector sum of\nindividual charges\nu Work done is just the sum of the\ncontribution of individual charges\nå\n=\n÷÷\nø\nö\nçç\nè\næ\n+\n+\n+\n=\ni\ni\ni\no\no\na\nr\nq\nkq\nr\nq\nr\nq\nr\nq\nkq\nU\n...\n3\n3\n2\n2\n1\n1\nFigure 8. Potential Energy for Several Point Charges.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics (pp 759) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R.\nA. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "u Sum extends over all pairs\nu A charge cannot interact with itself (i=j) not included\nu Avoid over-counting (i<j)\n24\nPotential Energy in assembling charges qi\nå\n<\n=\n÷÷\nø\nö\nçç\nè\næ\n+\n+\n+\n=\nj\ni\nij\ni\ni\nr\nq\nq\nk\nr\nq\nq\nr\nq\nq\nr\nq\nq\nk\nU\n...\n23\n3\n2\n13\n3\n1\n12\n2\n1\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "25\n1. Find the total potential energy of the system of three charges.\na\nke\nU\na\ne\ne\na\ne\ne\na\ne\ne\nk\nr\nq\nq\nr\nq\nq\nr\nq\nq\nk\nr\nq\nq\nk\nU\nj\ni\nij\nj\ni\n2\n2\n2\n23\n3\n2\n13\n3\n1\n12\n2\n1\n-\n=\n÷\nø\nö\nç\nè\næ\n+\n-\n+\n-\n=\n÷÷\nø\nö\nçç\nè\næ\n+\n+\n=\n= å\n<\n)\n)(\n(\n)\n)(\n(\n)\n)(\n(\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "2. A point charge 𝑞. = +4.00 𝑛𝐶is placed at the origin, a second point\ncharge 𝑞/ = −3.00 𝑛𝐶is placed on the x-axis at 𝑥= 20.0𝑐𝑚and a third\ncharge 𝑞0 = +2.00 𝑛𝐶is placed at 𝑥= 10.0 𝑐𝑚. What is the potential\nenergy of the system of three charges?\nSolution:\nSample Problems\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "Where\nV = electric potential (V)\nU =Potential energy (J)\nq = charge (C) \nr = distance of the charge (m)\nk = proportionality constant\nk = 9x109 Nm2/C2\nElectrical Potential, V, \nof a point charge is the \nratio of the total \nelectric potential \nenergy and the amount \nof the charge.\n \n! = #\n$ = %$\n& \n \n", "image": "slides/m5-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "Unit is volt.\nVolt  is defined as the change in \nelectrical potential difference that \noccurs when 1-coulomb charge \nexperiences a change in electrical \npotential energy of one joule.\nElectrical potential \ndifference or voltage \nchange (or simply Voltage) \nis defined as the change in \nelectrical energy a particle \nundergoes when moving \nbetween different points in \nspace. \n!\n1!!!\"# = 1!\"#$%\n!\"#$\"%&!\n!\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL DIFFERENCE\n𝑾𝒂→𝒃\n𝒒𝟎\n= −𝚫𝑼\n𝒒𝟎\n= −𝑼𝒃\n𝒒𝟎\n−𝑼𝒂\n𝒒𝟎\n= −𝑽𝒃−𝑽𝒂= 𝑽𝒂−𝑽𝒃= 𝑽𝒂𝒃\nThis means that the work done by the electric force\nto move a charge from point a to b is equal to the\npotential difference Vab.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL DIFFERENCE\nHOW DO BATTERIES WORK?\n§\nThe positive terminal of the battery\nrepresents\nthe\npoint\nwith\nhigh\npotential while the negative terminal\nrepresents\nthe\npoint\nwith\nlow\npotential.\n§\nThe voltage of the battery is the\npotential\ndifference\nbetween\nthe\nterminals. This potential difference is\nneeded for the charges to flow.\nFigure 9. How do batteries work. From \nhttp://www.qrg.northwestern.edu/projects/vss/docs/media/Power/battery.gif\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL DIFFERENCE\nElectrical sources such as  \nbatteries and generators\nsupply  a constant potential\ndifference.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "text": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL\nThe potential due to a single point \ncharge q is given by\n𝑽= 𝒌𝒒\n𝒓\nwhere:\n𝑽is electric potential in V\n𝒌=\n𝟏\n𝟒𝝅𝝐𝟎= 𝟗. 𝟎𝐱𝟏𝟎𝟗Nm2/C2\nr is the distance from q to the point \nwhere the potential is evaluated\nFor a collection of point charges, the\npotential is given by\n𝑽= 𝒌𝒒𝟏\n𝒓𝟏\n+ 𝒒𝟐\n𝒓𝟐\n+ ⋯\nFigure 10. Potential Energy for Several Point Charges.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With\nModern Physics (pp 777) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "text": "Solution:\n3. A point charge has a charge of 2.50×103..𝐶. What is the electric\npotential at a point 1.75 m away?\nSample Problem\n𝑉= 𝑈\n𝑞= 𝑘𝑞\n𝑟\n𝑉=\n() *+\"\n( -./ ) *+ #$$)\n*.1/ 2\n= 𝟎. 𝟏𝟐𝟗𝑽𝒐𝒍𝒕\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "text": "4. Determine the potential at a point 0.50m from \nA.  a  +20 μC point charge.\nB.  a -20 μC point charge.\nSolution: \nSample Problem\nA:#\n! = !\"\n! !\n!\n! =\n9!10!!!!/!!)(20!10!!!)\n0.50!!\n!\n!\n! = !. !!!!\"!!!#\n#\nB:#\n! = !\"\n! !\n!\n! =\n9!10!!!!/!!)(−20!10!!!)\n0.50!!\n!\n!\n! = −!. !!!!\"!!!#\n#\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "text": "5. In the Cartesian coordinate system, q1 =2x10-9C is at the origin \nand q2 = 6x10-9C is placed at (6,0) cm.. Find the net potential at \npoints A (0,7)cm.\nSolution:\nSample Problem\n(0,7)cm\n(6,0)cm\n(0,0)cm\nq2\nq1\nA\n!! = !!!\n!\n=\n9!10!!!!/!!)(2!10!!!)\n0.07!!\n= 257.14!!!\n!! = !!!\n!\n=\n9!10!!!!/!!)(6!10!!!)\n0.092!!\n= 586.96!!!\n!!\"#!\"!! = !! + !! = 257.14 + 586.96 !!\n!\n!!\"#!\"!! = !\"\". !\"!!!\n!\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "text": "EQUIPOTENTIAL SURFACE\nThe potential difference \nbetween 2 points is \nequal to zero. \nNo work is required to \nmove a charge from one \npoint to another on an \nequipotential surface.\nAn equipotential \nsurface is one on \nwhich all points are \nat the same \npotential.\nFigure 11. Equipotential Surface. From. https://s3-us-\nwest-2.amazonaws.com/courses-images/wp-\ncontent/uploads/sites/1989/2017/06/13225944/figur\ne-20-04-04a.jpeg\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/036.webp"}, {"page": 37, "text": "EQUIPOTENTIAL SURFACE\nAn equipotential surface is a three-dimensional surface on \nwhich the electric potential V is the same at every point. \nIf a test charge q0 is moved \nfrom point to point on such a \nsurface, the electric \npotential energy remains \nconstant.\nFigure 12. Equiotential surface. From. https://kaiserscience.files.wordpress.com/2016/01/equipotential-lines.gif\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/037.webp"}, {"page": 38, "text": "EQUIPOTENTIAL SURFACE\nAn equipotential surface must be perpendicular to  \nthe electric field at any point.\nFigure 13. Equipotential Surfaces. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics(pp755) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England:\nPearson.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/038.webp"}, {"page": 39, "text": "Sample Problem\nFor a single point charge with q = 4.0 × 10-9 C, sketch the equipotential surfaces \n(or lines in a plane containing the charge) corresponding to V1 = 10 V, V2 = 20 V, \nand V3 = 30 V.\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/039.webp"}, {"page": 40, "text": "Sample Problem\n! = !\"\n! !\n!\nSo,!!\n!\n! = !\"\n! !\n!\nSolution:\nFor: \nV1 = 10 V,  \nr = 3.6 m  \nV2 = 20 V,\nr = 1.8 m\nV3 = 30 V, \nr = 1.2 m\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/040.webp"}, {"page": 41, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and Engineers \nwith Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttp://www.qrg.northwestern.edu/projects/vss/docs/media/Power/b\nattery.gif\nhttps://s3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com/courses-images/wp-\ncontent/uploads/sites/1989/2017/06/13225944/figure-20-04-\n04a.jpeg\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m5-main/041.webp"}]}, {"id": "m5-dyn", "module": 5, "title": "Electric potential & energy", "variant": "dyn", "file": "Module 5 - Physics (Dyn).pdf", "sha256": "f9469f5f840bca4ff916c156b4a172e9ebc4a7aaed4187d24e6a83a6ac47e07c", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "", "image": "slides/m5-dyn/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "", "image": "slides/m5-dyn/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "", "image": "slides/m5-dyn/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "", "image": "slides/m5-dyn/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "", "image": "slides/m5-dyn/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "", "image": "slides/m5-dyn/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "", "image": "slides/m5-dyn/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "", "image": "slides/m5-dyn/008.webp"}]}, {"id": "m6-main", "module": 6, "title": "Capacitors & dielectrics", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 6 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "e13fd327f79b0c35aa0ce1e5bb7055f9b9eee8e3ed5435d1624237a15ef1ef60", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Capacitor Electrical Energy \nStorage and Dielectric\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Parallel Plate Capacitor \nand Dielectric\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDescribe the nature of parallel-plate capacitors\n§\nDiscuss dielectric materials.\n§\nDetermine the effects of adding dielectrics in a capacitor.\n§\nCalculate the amount of energy stored in a capacitor\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Figure 1 Types of Capacitors Source: Retrieved from.http://www.aliexpress.com and http://www.instructables.com\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "CAPACITOR AND ITS FUNCTION\nA capacitor is a passive two terminal \nelectrical component that stores potential \nenergy in an electric field.\nIt consists of two \nconductors \nseparated by a \ndielectric. \nIt is charged when \nconnected to a voltage \nsource and discharged \nwhen connected to a load.\nFigure 2. Assorted commercially available capacitors.. From. Sear's &\nZemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 793) by Young,\nH. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\nFigure 3. Conductive parallel plates. From.https://www.electronics-\ntutorials.ws/wp-content/uploads/2018/05/capacitor-cap1.gif\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "DIELECTRICS\nA\ndielectric\nis\na\nnon-conducting\nmaterial\nbetween the conducting plates of a capacitor.\nFUNCTIONS OF A DIELECTRIC\n§\nallows\nvery\nsmall\nseparation\nbetween\nconducting plates w/o actual contact\n§\nincreases the maximum possible potential\ndifference between the capacitor plates\n§\nincreases the capacitance of the capacitor\nFigure 4. A common type of capacitor uses dielectric sheets to separate the\nconductors.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics\n(pp 800) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England:\nPearson.\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "CAPACITANCE\n§ the plates have charges of \nequal magnitude but opposite \nsigns Q and -Q\n§ there’s uniform electric field \nE between the plates\n§ there’s potential difference \nVab between the plates\nFigure 5. Parallel plate capacitor . From. Physics for Scientists and\nEngineers with Modern Physics(pp 778) by Serway, R. A., &\nJewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "CAPACITANCE\nThe amount of charge that must be stored in the \nconductors to produce a certain potential \ndifference is called the capacitance and is given \nby:\n𝑪= 𝑸\n𝑽𝒂𝒃\nwhere:\n𝑪is the capacitance in farads (F)\n𝑸is the charge in coulombs (C)\n𝑽𝒂𝒃is the potential difference in volts (V)\nFigure 5. Parallel plate capacitor . From. Physics for Scientists and\nEngineers with Modern Physics(pp 778) by Serway, R. A., &\nJewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "FACTORS AFFECTING CAPACITANCE\nFor a parallel-plate capacitor consists of two plates each \nwith area A and separated by a distance d, the \ncapacitance is given by:\n𝑪= 𝝐𝟎\n𝑨\n𝒅\nwhere:\n𝑪is the capacitance in farads (F)\n𝑨is the area of the plates in square meters m2\n𝒅is the separation distance of the plates in meters (m)\n𝝐𝟎= 𝟖. 𝟖𝟓×𝟏𝟎\"𝟏𝟐𝑭/𝒎\nC is directly \nproportional with A\nC is inversely \nproportional with d\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "CAPACITANCE\nConceptual Question\nMany\ncomputer\nkeyboard\nbuttons\nare\nconstructed of capacitors as shown in the figure.\nWhen a key is pushed down, the soft insulator\nbetween the movable plate and the fixed plate is\ncompressed. What happens to the capacitance\nwhen the key is pressed?\nAns: It increases.\nFigure 7. Computer Keyboard Button. From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with\nModern Physics(pp 780) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage\nLearning.\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "11\nA\nd\nDepends only on\nthe geometry\nØ ↑A, ↑ C\nØ ↑d, ↓C\nØ ↓ A, ↓ C\nØ ↓ d, ↑ C\no\nE\ne\ns\n=\nA\nQ\n=\ns\nA\nQ\nE\n0\ne\n=\nEd\nVab =\nA\nQd\nV\no\nab\ne\n=\nd\nA\nC\no\ne\n=\nab\nV\nQ\nC =\nFigure 8. Electric Field Lines with Vacuum between Plates .. From. Sear's\n& Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 801) by Young,\nH. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "Table 1. Values of Dielectric Constant.. From. Sear's &\nZemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp\n801) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A.\n&\nA. Lewis\nFord(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "for other Dielectric Material \n𝑪= 𝒌𝝐𝟎\n𝑨\n𝒅\nwhere: 𝒌= dielectric constant \n", "image": "slides/m6-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "1. A parallel plate capacitor has a capacitance of 1.0 F. If the plates \nare 1.0mm apart, what is the area of the plates?\nANSWER: 1.1x108 m2\n2. The plates of a parallel plate capacitor in vacuum are 5.0mm \napart and 2.0m2 in area. A potential difference of 10,000 V is \napplied across the capacitor. Compute (a) the capacitance (b) the \ncharge on each plate; and (c) the magnitude of electric field \nANSWER: (a) 0.00354µF (b) 35.4µC (c) 2.0x106 N/C\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "ENERGY STORAGE IN CAPACITORS\nThe electric potential energy stored in a charged capacitor is just\nequal to the amount of work required to charge it. It is given by:\n𝑼= 𝟏\n𝟐𝑪𝑽𝒂𝒃\n𝟐= 𝟏\n𝟐𝑸𝑽𝒂𝒃\nwhere:\n𝑼is the electric potential energy in joules (J)\n𝑪is the capacitance in farads (F)\n𝑸is the charge of the capacitor in coulombs (C)\n𝑽is the potential difference (voltage) in volts (V)\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "3. A 5.80 𝜇𝐹, parallel-plate, air capacitor has a plate separation of 5.00 𝑚𝑚and \nis charged to a potential difference of 4.00×10% 𝑉. Calculate the energy stored \nin the capacitor.\nANSWER: 0.464 J\n4. How much charge does a battery have to supply to a 5.0 𝜇𝐹capacitor to \ncreate a potential difference of 1.5 𝑉across its plates?\nANSWER: 7.5 𝝁C\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: \nCengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttps://www.electronics-tutorials.ws/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2018/05/capacitor-cap1.gif\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "Capacitors in Series \nand Parallel\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nAnalyze capacitors connected in a network\n§\nCalculate the total capacitance of capacitors in a network\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "Circuit symbols for \ncapacitors, batteries, and \nswitches. \nFigure 9 Computer Keyboard Button. From. Physics for Scientists\nand Engineers with Modern Physics(pp 782) by Serway, R. A., &\nJewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "For capacitors in series:\n𝑸𝑻= 𝑸𝟏= 𝑸𝟐= 𝑸𝟑\n𝑽𝒂𝒃= 𝑽𝟏+ 𝑽𝟐+ 𝑽𝟑\n𝟏\n𝑪𝑻\n= 𝟏\n𝑪𝟏\n+ 𝟏\n𝑪𝟐\n+ 𝟏\n𝑪𝟑\nFigure 10. Capacitors in series. From. \nhttps://cdn.shortpixel.ai/client/q_glossy,ret_img,w_228,h_183/https://electronicspani.com/\nwp-content/uploads/2014/11/Capacitors-in-Series.gif\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "For capacitors in parallel:\n𝑽𝒂𝒃= 𝑽𝟏= 𝑽𝟐= 𝑽𝟑\n𝑸𝑻= 𝑸𝟏+ 𝑸𝟐+ 𝑸𝟑\n𝑪𝑻= 𝑪𝟏+ 𝑪𝟐+ 𝑪𝟑\nFigure11. Capacitors in \nparallel..From.https://tse3.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.9OfBunwr\ngzrkznVZBk1DCQAAAA&pid=Api&P=0&w=322&h=158\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "Conceptual Question\nYou want to connect a 4 𝜇𝐹capacitor and an 8 𝜇𝐹\ncapacitor. What type of connection will the 4 𝜇𝐹\ncapacitor have a greater potential difference across it\nthan the 8 𝜇𝐹capacitor?\nAns: series connection\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "1. Two capacitors, 𝐶& = 6.0 𝜇𝐹and 𝐶% = 3.0 𝜇𝐹are connected to a 18.0 𝑉\nbattery. Find the total capacitance and find the charge and potential \ndifference for each capacitor when the two capacitors are connected (a) in \nseries and (b) in parallel\nANSWER: (a) 2 µF (b) 9 µF\n2. Two capacitors, 𝐶& = 5.0 𝜇𝐹and 𝐶% = 12.0 𝜇𝐹are connected in parallel to a \n9.0 𝑉battery. Find (a) the total capacitance and (b) the potential difference \nacross each capacitor.\nANSWER: (a) 17 µF (b) 9.0 V\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "Find the (a) total capacitance, (b) \npotential difference and (c) charge \nacross each capacitor  for the \ncombination shown in the figure. \nPotential difference between pt. a \nand b is Vab = 28 V.\nFigure 12. Capacitors in series and parallel.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics \n(pp 796) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A.  &  A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "APPLICATIONS OF CAPACITORS\nprotection when there are power \nsurges\nprovides charge at a greater rate\nFigure 13. Application of capacitors. From. \nhttps://magazine.odroid.com/article/odroid-c2-\npower-off-wake-using-simple-gpio-button/\nFigure 14. TV Remote control. \nFrom.https://tse3.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.J5mtsttLmDjNQI17QLBHp\nwHaHa&pid=Api&P=0&w=300&h=300\nFigure 15. Camera with flash.  From. \nhttps://c.pxhere.com/photos/68/0e/camera_digital_equipment_female_girl_isolated_lens\n_people-1260303.jpg!d\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics \nTenth Edition. Australia: Cengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern \nPhysics. England: Pearson.\nhttps://cdn.shortpixel.ai/client/q_glossy,ret_img,w_228,h_183/https://electronicspani.com/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2014/11/Capacitors-in-Series.gif\nhttps://tse3.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.9OfBunwrgzrkznVZBk1DCQAAAA&pid=Api&P=0&w=322&h=\n158\nhttps://tse3.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.J5mtsttLmDjNQI17QLBHpwHaHa&pid=Api&P=0&w=300&h=300\nhttps://c.pxhere.com/photos/68/0e/camera_digital_equipment_female_girl_isolated_l\nens_people-1260303.jpg!d\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m6-main/027.webp"}]}, {"id": "m6-dyn", "module": 6, "title": "Capacitors & dielectrics", "variant": "dyn", "file": "Module 6 - Physics (Dyn).pdf", "sha256": "bf6e80d35db6a21c1d824d698e2ac1ea20fbdab5d86e8371dbf7636d83277383", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "", "image": "slides/m6-dyn/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "", "image": "slides/m6-dyn/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "", "image": "slides/m6-dyn/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "", "image": "slides/m6-dyn/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "", "image": "slides/m6-dyn/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "", "image": "slides/m6-dyn/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "", "image": "slides/m6-dyn/007.webp"}]}, {"id": "m7-main", "module": 7, "title": "Current & DC circuits", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 7 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "db8c85bffded8e35fe01c7bbc06f445cca3a8d051539779683cf24e98aa86aad", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Electric Current \nand DC Circuits\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Current, Resistance \nand Ohm’s Law\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDiscuss the concepts of electric current, drift velocity and current density\n§\nDifferentiate the resistivity of conductor and insulators\n§\nState Ohm’s law and solve problems involving Ohm’s law\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "u Motion of charge from one region to another\nu In electrostatics, electric field everywhere\nwithin a conductor is zero\nu no current\nu In general, free electrons move randomly in a\nconductor\nu But since the movement is random there is no\nnet flow in any direction →no current\n4\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Electric\ncurrent\nis\nthe\nrate\nof\nflow\nof\nelectrons in a circuit.\nThe unit of current, called ampere, is named\nin\nhonor\nof\nAndre’\nMarie\nAmpere\n(1775-\n1836), a French physicist. It is expressed in terms\nof the number of coulombs per second going past a\ngiven point on a wire.\n1 A = 1 Coulomb/second = 1C/s\nThe flow of charge  in \na wire is called  \ncurrent.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "6\nFigure 1. Random motion of the charged particles.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University\nPhysics With Modern Physics (pp 819) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A.\n&\nA. Lewis\nFord(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "Direction of current\nu νd – result of acceleration of the electrons due\nto the electric field and the repeated collisions\nwith the atoms.\n7\ncoll\nd\ntime\ncollision\nE\nto\ndue\na\nv\nt\n´\n=\n!\n!\nFigure 2. Current produce by a) a positive charge and b) a negative charge.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics (pp 820) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "Direction of current\nu Direction of I is in the same direction where there\nis a flow of positive charge – conventional current\n8\nFigure 2. Current produce by a) a positive charge and b) a negative charge.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics (pp 820) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "Definition of Current\nu Current is not a vector\nu SI Unit: Ampere (A) or (C/s)\n9\nØ Net\ncharge\nflowing\nthrough the area per unit\ntime\ndt\ndQ\nI =\nFigure 3. The current I. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 825) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A.\nLewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "The amount of  \nelectric current  \nflowing per unit  \ncross-sectional  \narea of a material.\nFigure 4. Current Density. http://s3-ap-southeast-\n1.amazonaws.com/subscriber.images/physics/2016/10/27125734/Current-Density.png\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "The average velocity of the\nfree charges is\ncalled the\ndrift velocity\nThe direction of drift velocity is opposite to  \nthe electric field for electrons.\nThe electric field in the conductor causes the\nelectrons to drift in the direction opposite to the\nfield, since they are negative.\nFigure 5. Drift Velocity. From. https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.sFSTlowlBvCRq9CZ9O0LIQHaD5&pid=Api&P=0&w=344&h=182\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "u Current density – current per cross sectional area\nu Units: A/m2\nu If the charges are negative, it moves opposite to E\nbut the current is still along E\nu I and J don’t depend on the sign of the charge\n12\nCurrent, Drift Velocity and Current Density\nd\nnqv\nA\nI\nJ\n=\n=\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "Current, Drift Velocity and Current Density\n13\nVector current density is\nalways in the direction\nof the field\nA\nv\nq\nn\ndt\ndQ\nI\nD\n=\n=\nD\nv\nq\nn\nA\nI\nJ\n=\n=\nD\nv\nnq\nJ\n!\n!\n=\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "Current and Current Density\nu Current due to an ensemble of charges is the\nsum\nof\nthe\nindividual\ncurrents\nfor\neach\ncharge\nu Similarly, total current density is just the sum\nof the individual current densities\n14\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "Table 1. Effect  of electric current of  human body. From. http://2.bp.blogspot.com/-oV6vCLqwnc8/TpFltQmBw9I/AAAAAAAAAEY/qHdnVQwSfI4/s1600/Current+Effects.JPG\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "The current density J in a wire is given by 50.0A/m2i. If the drift\nvelocity of the charge carriers (q = -0.1C) is vd= –2.0 ×10-4m/s\ni, what is the concentration of the carriers?\ndv\nq\nn\nJ\n|\n|\n=\ndv\nq\nJ\nn\n|\n|\n=\n®\n3\n6\n4\n2\n10\n5\n2\n10\n0\n2\n1\n0\n0\n50\n-\n-\n´\n=\n´\n-\n=\nm\ns\nm\nC\nm\nA\nn\n.\n/\n.\n|\n.\n|\n/\n.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "1. How much current is flowing when 29.3 C of charge pass a point in 7.84 \nseconds?\nAnswer: I = 3.74 A\n2.  A calculator draws a current of 0.01 A for 60 seconds. Calculate the \nmagnitude of charge. \nAnswer: Q = 0.6 C\n3. 15 C of charge flow past a given point in a wire in 3 seconds. What is the \ncurrent in Amperes?\nAnswer: I = 5 A\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "Resistivity, is commonly  \nsymbolized by the Greek letter  \nrho, ρ, is quantitatively equal to  \nthe resistance, R, of a specimen  \nsuch as a wire, multiplied by its  \ncross-sectional area, A, and  \ndivided by its length, l.\nWhere\nρ = Resistivity, ohm-meter (Ωm)  \nR = resistance, Ohm (Ω)\nA = wire cross-sectional area(m2)  \nl = length (m)\nFigure 5. Georg Simon Ohm. From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers\nwith Modern Physics(pp 814) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019).\nAustralia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "The value of resistivity depends  \nalso on the temperature of the  \nmaterial.\nWhere:\nΔR – change in resistance, Ω\nR - resistance of the substance, Ω  \nΔT – change in temperature, ºC\nα – temperature coefficient of resistance of the material (1/0C)\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "Table 2. Resistivities and Temperature Coefficients. From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp\n814) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "- Inverse of resistivity\nHow\nmuch\nthe\nmaterial\npermits\nflow of current\nHow much the\nmaterial resists\nflow of current\nr\ns\n1\n=\nu Good conductor: ↑σ, ↓ρ\nu Good insulator: ↓σ, ↑ρ\nu Conductivity is the electric analogy of thermal conductivity\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "Resistor – is a passive\nelectrical component that\nprovides resistance in the\nelectric\ncurrent\nin\nthe\ncircuit. Or in other words,\nit limits the current in the\ncircuit.\nFigure 6. Color Coding for Resistors. From. \nhttp://www.circuitstoday.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/03/6-\nBand_Resistor_Color_Code-782x1024.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "Ohm’s law:\nρ is constant and independent of the electric field\nV and total current I is easier to measure than E and J\nJ\nE\n!\n!\nr\n=\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "USEFUL EQUATIONS:\nEL\nV =\nJ\nE\nr\n=\nI\nA\nL\nV\nr\n=\nFigure 7. Current density is uniform in any cross section. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 825) by\nYoung, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "- The ratio of V per current I is called the resistance of the \nconductor:\n- The relationship of the resistivity to the potential is:\nI\nV\nR =\nIR\nV =\nA\nL\nR\nr\n=\nDepends on the dimensions and material\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "- Resistance also varies with temperature \n- Ro is the resistivity at a reference temperature To (0oC or 20oC)\nα is the temperature coefficient of resistance\n( )\n(\n)\n[\n]\no\no\nT\nT\nR\nT\nR\n-\n+\n=\na\n1\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "The basic law of current flow is Ohm's law. It\nis named after its discoverer, the German\nphysicist Georg Ohm.\nThe ratio of the potential difference to the  \ncurrent is called the resistance of the  \nconductor, and is measured in ohms,  \nsymbol Ω.\nThe electric current flowing  \nthrough a metallic  \nconductor is directly  \nproportional to the  \npotential difference  \nbetween its ends, as long  as \nthe temperature stays  the\nsame.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "The relationship between voltage, current \nand resistance can be simply described by \nwhat we call Ohm’s Law. \nOhm’s Law states that: “the current flowing \nin a circuit is directly proportional to the \nvoltage applied and inversely proportional to \nthe resistance, given that the temperature is \nheld constant”\nFigure 8. Ohm’s Law. From. https://cdn.instructables.com/FV7/BX1U/JN8YDEFY/FV7BX1UJN8YDEFY.LARGE.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "A light bulb has a resistance of 250Ω. Find the current in it when it is placed in a110V  \ncircuit.\nGiven:\nV = 110V\nR = 250Ω\nRequired:\nI = ?\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "text": "An electric toaster is connected to a power source of 220V. The current through it is  \n0.5A. What is its resistance?\nGiven:\nV = 220V\nI = 0.50A\nRequired:\nR = ?\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "text": "What potential difference must be applied across a 1200Ω resistor in order that the  \nresulting current be 30 milliamperes?\nGiven:\nR = 1200Ω\nI = 30 mA\nRequired:\nV = ?\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and Engineers with \nModern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics \nWith Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttp://s3-ap-southeast-\n1.amazonaws.com/subscriber.images/physics/2016/10/27125734/Current-\nDensity.png\nhttps://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.sFSTlowlBvCRq9CZ9O0LIQHaD5&pid=Api&P\n=0&w=344&h=182\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "text": "http://2.bp.blogspot.com/-\noV6vCLqwnc8/TpFltQmBw9I/AAAAAAAAAEY/qHdnVQwSfI4/s1600/Current+Eff\nects.JPG\nhttp://www.circuitstoday.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/03/6-\nBand_Resistor_Color_Code-782x1024.jpg\nhttps://cdn.instructables.com/FV7/BX1U/JN8YDEFY/FV7BX1UJN8YDEFY.LARGE.\njpg\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "text": "Power, Energy, EMF and \nInternal Resistance\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "text": "OBJECTIVE\n§\nSolve the energy and power in an electric circuit.\n§\nUnderstand the effect of emf and internal resistance in a circuit \n", "image": "slides/m7-main/036.webp"}, {"page": 37, "text": "Figure 9. Symbols for Circuit Diagrams .From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 831) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/037.webp"}, {"page": 38, "text": "Electrical power is  \nthe rate at which  \nelectrical energy is  \nbeing used.\nP = Power, W or \nJ/s  V = Potential,\nV\nI = Current, A\nFigure 10. Ohms’ Law Wheel. From. http://www.reuk.co.uk/OtherImages/ohms-law.gif\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/038.webp"}, {"page": 39, "text": "1. Find the power consumed by a 120 Ω light bulb when the current  \nthrough it is 0.9 A.\nGiven:    R = 120 Ω\nI = 0.9 A\nRequired:\nP\nSolution:\nP = I2R\nP = (0.9 A)2 (120Ω)\nP = 97.2 Watts\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/039.webp"}, {"page": 40, "text": "2. The power dissipated by an electric appliance is 30W. What is the \ncurrent flowing  through it if it is connected to a power source of \n110V?\nGiven: R = 30 W,  V =110V\nRequired:\nI\nSolution:\nP = VI\nI = P / V\nI = (30W/110V)   I = 0.27 A\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/040.webp"}, {"page": 41, "text": "u A\nsource\nof\nelectromotive\nforce (emf) provides energy\nu For an emf source that can be\ndescribed\nby\nan\ninternal\nresistance r\nab\nIV\nP =\nIr\nVab\n-\n= E\nFigure 11. Real Source of emf and a bulb.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With\nModern Physics (pp 831) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England:\nPearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/041.webp"}, {"page": 42, "text": "Net power\ninput\nto\nthe source\nRate\nat\nwhich\nelectric energy is\ndissipated\nin\nthe\ninternal resistance\nRate of conversion \nfrom non-electrical  \nto electrical energy\nu If\nthe\nemf\nsource\nis\nconnected to a larger emf\nsource (battery charging)\n(\n)\nr\nI\nI\nIr\nI\nP\n2\n+\n=\n+\n=\nE\nE\nFigure 11. Power input\nto\na\nsource..\nFrom.\nSear's\n&\nZemansky's\nUniversity Physics With\nModern\nPhysics\n(pp\n835) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A.\nLewis\nFord(2016).\nEngland: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/042.webp"}, {"page": 43, "text": "43\n3. Given the figure, what are the \nrates of energy conversion and \ndissipation and the net power \noutput of the battery?\nFigure 12. Power input to a source.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 836)\nby Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/043.webp"}, {"page": 44, "text": "Solution: \na) Energy conversion:\n= 24 𝑊\nb) Energy dissipation:\nc) Net power output:\n)\n2\n)(\n12\n(\nA\nV\nI =\nE\nW\nA\nr\nI\n8\n)\n2\n(\n)\n2\n(\n2\n2\n=\nW\n=\nW\nW\nW\nr\nI\nI\n16\n)\n8\n(\n)\n24\n(\n2\n=\n-\n=\n-\nE\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/044.webp"}, {"page": 45, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: \nCengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttp://www.reuk.co.uk/OtherImages/ohms-law.gif\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/045.webp"}, {"page": 46, "text": "Series Parallel \nResistors\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/046.webp"}, {"page": 47, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nGive the characteristics of series and parallel resistive circuits\n§\nCompute the equivalent resistance, current, and emf in series or parallel or series-parallel \nconnection\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/047.webp"}, {"page": 48, "text": "Electric circuit is a path of an electric current.\nThe term is usually taken to mean a continuous path composed of conductors and\nconducting devices and including a source of electromotive force (emf) that drives the\ncurrent around the circuit. A circuit of this type is termed a closed circuit, and a circuit\nin which the current path is not continuous is called an open circuit.\nA short circuit is a closed circuit in which a direct connection is made, with no  \nappreciable resistance, inductance, or capacitance, between the terminals of the  \nsource of electromotive force.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/048.webp"}, {"page": 49, "text": "Electric circuits with only  a single \npath for  electricity to flow along  \nare called series circuits.\nFigure13. Resistors in Series. From. https://www.wikihow.com/images/thumb/8/8e/Calculate-Total-Resistance-\nin-Circuits-Step-1-Version-2.jpg/aid487580-v4-728px-Calculate-Total-Resistance-in-Circuits-Step-1-Version-2.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/049.webp"}, {"page": 50, "text": "Figure 14. Resistance in Series Equations. From. \nhttps://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/airplane/Images/ohmrser.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/050.webp"}, {"page": 51, "text": "Electric circuits with  multiple \npathways for  electricity to flow \nalong  are called parallel  circuits.\nFigure 15.Resistors in Parallel. From.  https://www.wikihow.com/images/thumb/0/0a/Calculate-Total-Resistance-in-\nCircuits-Step-5-Version-2.jpg/aid487580-v4-728px-Calculate-Total-Resistance-in-Circuits-Step-5-Version-2.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/051.webp"}, {"page": 52, "text": "Figure 16. Resistance in Parallel Equations. From. http://www.grc.nasa.gov/WWW/K-\n12/airplane/Images/ohmrpar.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/052.webp"}, {"page": 53, "text": "1. Find the total resistance of the \ngiven circuit.\nANSWER: 18 kΩ\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/053.webp"}, {"page": 54, "text": "2. Three resistors 2Ω, 3Ω, and 5Ω are connected in \n(a)series (b) parallel.  \nFind the equivalent resistance, the current in each resistor \nand the  potential difference across each resistor if the \nnetwork is connected  to a power source of 50V.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/054.webp"}, {"page": 55, "text": "a) RESISTORS IN SERIES\nRT\nI1, I2, I3\nV1, V2, V3=?\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/055.webp"}, {"page": 56, "text": "b) RESISTORS IN PARALLEL\nRT, \nI1, I2, I3, \nV1, V2, V3=?\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/056.webp"}, {"page": 57, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttps://i.ytimg.com/vi/ppZMG7y4kg8/maxresdefault.jpg\nhttps://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/airplane/Images/ohmrser.jpg\nhttp://www.grc.nasa.gov/WWW/K-12/airplane/Images/ohmrpar.jpg\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/057.webp"}, {"page": 58, "text": "Kirchhoff’s Law\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/058.webp"}, {"page": 59, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nExplain Kirchhoff’s loop and current rules.\n§\nUse Kirchhoff’s rules in analyzing and solving resistive circuits.\n§\nAnalyze circuits that include both a resistor and a capacitor\n§\nSolve RC circuits problems\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/059.webp"}, {"page": 60, "text": "Kirchhoff’s Voltage Law(KVL): The algebraic sum of the \npotential differences in any closed loop must equal to zero.\nKirchhoff’s Current Law (KCL): The algebraic sum of the \ncurrents into any junction is equal to zero. Or in other words, \ncurrent ENTERING into a junction is equal to the CURRENT \nleaving the junction.\n! 𝑉= 0\n! 𝐼= 0 ; 𝐼!\"#!$%\"& = 𝐼'!()%\"&\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/060.webp"}, {"page": 61, "text": "Two networks that cannot be \nreduced to simple series-parallel \ncombinations of resistors.\nFigure 17 a) & b) Two networks that can not be reduced. From. . Real Source of emf and a bulb.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University\nPhysics With Modern Physics (pp 855) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/061.webp"}, {"page": 62, "text": "A junction, also called a node\nor branch point, is  a point \nwhere three or more \nconductors meet\nA loop is any closed \nconducting path\nFigure 18. Kirchhoff’s junction /loop rule. . From. . Real Source of emf and a bulb.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With\nModern Physics (pp 856) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/062.webp"}, {"page": 63, "text": "1. The algebraic sum of the currents into any junction is zero å\n= 0\nI\nA sign convention:\n0\n3\n2\n1\n=\n-\n+\nI\nI\nI\nA current heading towards a junction, is considered to be positive,\nA current heading away from a junction, is considered to be negative\nBe aware that all the junction equations for a circuit may not be \nindependent of each other\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/063.webp"}, {"page": 64, "text": "2. The algebraic sum of the potential differences in any loop including those associated with emfs and those \nof resistive elements must equal zero\nå\n= 0\nV\nProcedures to apply this rule:\nPick a direction for the current in each branch\nIf you picked the wrong direction, the current would come out negative\nFigure 19. Multiloop Circuits. From. http://buphy.bu.edu/~duffy/PY106/10b.GIF\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/064.webp"}, {"page": 65, "text": "KIRCHOFF’S RULE\nSign conventions for the loop rule \n• Use these sign conventions \nZhen \\oX appl\\ Kirchhoff¶s \nloop rule.  \n \n• In each part of the figure, \n³TraYel´ is Whe direcWion WhaW \nwe imagine going around the \nloop, which is not necessarily \nthe direction of the current. \n© 2016 Pearson Education Inc. \n", "image": "slides/m7-main/065.webp"}, {"page": 66, "text": "Kirchoff’s Rules\nPick a direction for traversing a loop – this direction must be the same for all loops\nNote that there is a third loop along the outside branches\nAs with the junction equations not all the loop equations will be independent of each \nother.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/066.webp"}, {"page": 67, "text": "Kirchoff’s Rules\nStarting at any point on the loop add the emfs and IR terms\nAn IR term is negative if we traverse it in the same sense as the current that is going through it, otherwise it is \npositive\nAn emf is considered to be positive if we go in the direction - to +, otherwise it is negative\nNeed to have as many independent equations as there are \nunknowns\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/067.webp"}, {"page": 68, "text": "Kirchoff’s Rules\nFor loop I we have\n0\n3\n1\n4\n3\n2\n1\n1\n1\n=\n-\n+\n-\n-\n-\ne\ne\nR\nI\nR\nI\nR\nI\nFor loop II we have\n0\n3\n2\n4\n3\n3\n2\n=\n+\n-\n+\n-\ne\ne\nR\nI\nR\nI\nJunction equation at a gives us\n0\n3\n2\n1\n=\n-\n-\nI\nI\nI\nWe now have three equations for the three unknown currents\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/068.webp"}, {"page": 69, "text": "ANS: -0.33 A \n1. A single loop \ncircuit contains two \nresistors and two \nbatteries as shown \nin the figure. \nFind the current  in \nthe circuit. \nFigure 19. Sample Problem. . From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp\n845) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/069.webp"}, {"page": 70, "text": "Calculate the currents I1, I2\nand I3 in the 3 branches of\nthe circuit.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/070.webp"}, {"page": 71, "text": "Calculate the currents I1, I2 and I3 in the 3  \nbranches of the circuit.\n1.\nLabel the currents\n1.\nUse I1, I2, I3 for the current in 3 \nseparate  branches.\n2.\nThe directions of I2 and I3 are chosen as \nsuch  since current tends to move away \nfrom the  positive terminal of a battery.\n3.\nDirection of I1 is not obvious so an \narbitrary  direction is assumed. If the \ncomputed I1 is  negative, then the \ndirection should be the  opposite.\n2. Identify the unknowns (I1, I2 and I3)\nThere are 3 unknowns so 3 equations are  \nneeded to solve.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/071.webp"}, {"page": 72, "text": "(Eq. 1)\n3. Junction rule\nJunction (a):  \nJunction (d):\nI3 = I1 +I2\nsame equation\n4. Loop rule\nChoose any 2 loops. (3 loops possible)\nLoop ahdcba:\nVha=(-I1)(30Ω) = -30I1  \nVcd= 45 V\nVdh = 0  \nVbc= -I3(1Ω) = -I3\nVab= -I3(40Ω) = -40I3\nVha+ Vdh + Vcd + Vbc + Vab= 0\n-30I1 + 0 + 45 + (-I3) + (-40I3) =0\n-30I1 + 45 - 41I3) =0\n(Eq. 2)\nCalculate the currents I1, I2 and I3 in the \n3  branches of the circuit.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/072.webp"}, {"page": 73, "text": "4. Loop rule\nLoop ahdefga::\nVdh =0\nVfe= I2(1Ω) =\nVha=(-I1)(30Ω) = -30I1  \nVed= I2(20Ω) = 20I2\nI2\nVgf= -80\nVga =0\nVha+ Vdh + Ved  + Vfe + Vgf + Vag=\n0\n-30I1 + 0 + 20I2 + I2 + (-80) + 0 = 0\n-30I1  + 21I2  - 80)= 0\n(Eq. 3)\n5. Solve the equations\nI2 = (80+30I1)/21 = 3.8 +1.4I1\nI3 = (45 – 30I11)/41 = 1.1 –0.73I1\nCalculate the currents I1, I2 and I3 in the \n3  branches of the circuit.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/073.webp"}, {"page": 74, "text": "5. Solve the equations\nI2 = (80+30I1)/21 = 3.8 +1.4I1\n(Eq. 4)\nI3 = (45 – 30I11)/41 = 1.1 – 0.73I1  \nI1 = I3 – I2\n(Eq. 5)\n(Eq. 6)\nSubstituting Eq. 4) and 5 to Eq. 6,\nI1= I3– I2= 1.1 – 0.73I1– 3.8 + 1.4I1 I1\n= -0.87A\nThe\nnegative\nsign\nindicates\nthat\nthe\nactual\ndirection is opposite to what was initially assumed.\n2\n3\nSolving for I\nand I :\nI2 = 3.8 + 1.4I1= 3.8 +1.4(-0.87)\nI2=2.6A\nI3 = 1.1 – 0.73I1 = 1.1 –0.73(-0.87)\nI3=1.7A\nCalculate the currents I1, I2 and I3 in the \n3  branches of the circuit.\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/074.webp"}, {"page": 75, "text": "Capacitors and \nresistors are  \noften found \ntogether in a  \ncircuit. Such RC \ncircuits are  \nused to control \na car’s  \nwindshield \nwipers and \ntiming  of traffic \nlights, camera \nflashes,  heart \npacemakers, \nand many  other \nelectronic\ndevices.\nFigure 20 a) Capacitor initially uncharged b) Charging the Capacitor. From. Real Source of emf and a bulb.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics (pp 865) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\nFigure 21 a)  Graph of current  versus time. b) Graph of capacitor charge versus \ntime.Physics With Modern Physics (pp 865) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A.  &  A. Lewis \nFord(2016). England: Pearson\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/075.webp"}, {"page": 76, "text": "CHARGING OF THE CAPACITOR\nWhere\nQ0 = maximum charge, C  \nR = resistance, Ω\nC = capacitance, F  \ne = 2.718\nt = time, s\nτ =RC= time constant , s\nWhere\nVC = potential difference across capacitor, V\nξ = emf of the battery\nQ = charge on the capacitor, C\nR= resistance, Ω  C \n= capacitance, F  e \n= 2.718\nt = time, s\nτ =RC= time constant , s (the time it takes to for\nthe voltage to reach 63% of its maximum value)\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/076.webp"}, {"page": 77, "text": "For the RC circuit, the voltage  \non the capacitor decreases  \nwith time after the switch S is  \nclosed.\nThe charge on the\ncapacitor  follows \nthe same curve\nsince  Q αV.\nFigure 22 a) Capacitor initially charged b) Discharging the Capacitor. From. Real Source of emf and a\nbulb.. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 867) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\nFigure 23 a)  Graph of current  versus time. b) Graph of capacitor \ncharge versus time.Physics With Modern Physics (pp 865) by \nYoung, H. D., Freedman, R. A.  &  A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: \nPearson\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/077.webp"}, {"page": 78, "text": "DISCHARGING OF THE CAPACITOR\nWhere\nQ0 = maximum charge, C  \nR = resistance, Ω\nC = capacitance, F  \ne = 2.718\nt = time, s\nτ =RC= time constant , s\nWhere\nV0 =initial voltage across capacitor, V  \nR= resistance, Ω\nC = capacitance, F\ne = 2.718\nt = time, s\nτ =RC= time constant , s (the time it takes for the\nvoltage across the capacitor to drop to 37% of its initial  \nvalue)\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/078.webp"}, {"page": 79, "text": "If a charged capacitor, C=35μF is connected to a resistance R = 120 Ω , how much  \ntime will elapse until the voltage falls to 10% of its original (maximum) value?\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/079.webp"}, {"page": 80, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: \nCengage Learning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's\nUniversity Physics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nhttp://buphy.bu.edu/~duffy/PY106/10b.GIF\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m7-main/080.webp"}]}, {"id": "m7-dyn", "module": 7, "title": "Current & DC circuits", "variant": "dyn", "file": "Module 7 - Physics (Dyn).pdf", "sha256": "2d0111e2ed21c18307b6999502ab2c72027ce14294bc24f9189334740afcaff1", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "", "image": "slides/m7-dyn/016.webp"}]}, {"id": "m8-main", "module": 8, "title": "Magnetic fields", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 8 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "4d3800ee97e5810f1750c15fd02d6d9898ab54d6627587c9737307c71452fbfe", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Magnetic Field \nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Force on a Moving \nCharge\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§ Analyze magnetic forces on current-carrying conductors\n§ Explain how current loops behave when placed in a magnetic field\n§ Explain why currents in the same direction attract while wires carrying opposing  currents repel\n§ Discuss the nature of the force that a moving charged particle experiences in a  magnetic field\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Any magnet, whether it is in the shape of a\nbar or a horseshoe, has 2 ends or faces\ncalled poles.\nThe pole of a magnet that points toward\nthe geographic north is called the north\npole.\nThe other pole that points the south is the\nsouth pole.\nLike poles of a magnet repel while unlike\npoles attract.\nFigure 1 Two bar magnets a) Opposite poles attracts b) Like poles repel. From. Sear's &\nZemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 884) by Young, H. D., Freedman,\nR. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "In the 5th century BC, the Greeks already knew \nthat there are some rocks that attracts bits or \niron. \nAnd they are very plentiful in the district of \nMagnesia and so that’s where the name \n“magnets” and “magnetism” came from. The \nrocks contains what we call a “magnetite”\nMagnetite has two places of maximum attraction \nof what we call, POLES.\nFigure 2. Magnetite.From. \nhttps://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.35wltD4EAIq_oicACd5gdAHaEo&pid=Api&P=0&w=262&h=164\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "If you split a magnet, you  \nwon’t get isolated north and  \nsouth poles. New magnets  \nare produced with a north  \nand a south pole.\nhttps://vercauterendavid.files.wordpress.com/2017/12/chapter\n-27.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "Either pole of a magnet attracts an \nunmagnetized object that contains iron \nsuch as a nail\nPermanent magnets (magnetites) were found \nalso to exert forces on pieces of iron that were \nnot magnetized. \nIt was discovered that when an iron rod is \nbrought in contact with a natural magnet, the rod \nalso becomes magnetized. \nWhen such a rod is suspended by a string from its \ncenter, it tends to line up in a north-south \ndirection.\nThe needle of an ordinary compass is just a piece \nof magnetized iron. \nFigure 4.. Either pole of a magnet attracts an unmagnetized object that contains\niron such as a nail. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern\nPhysics (pp 884) by Young, H. D., Freedman, R. A.\n&\nA. Lewis Ford(2016).\nEngland: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "The direction of the magnetic field is tangent to  \nthe field line at any point\nThe number of lines per unit area is  \nproportional to the strength of the magnetic  \nfield.\nThe direction of the magnetic field at a given  \npoint can be defined as the direction of that the  \nnorth pole of a compass needle would point if  \nplaced at that point.\nFigure 5. Magnetic Field Lines. From. https://www.polytechnichub.com/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2018/08/magnetic-flux-lines.jpg\nFigure 6. Using compass to show the magnetic field. From. \nhttps://tse2.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.JYb8OGDWupgs93Ey2e8L6QAA\nAA&pid=Api&P=0&w=213&h=177\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "The earth acts like\na huge magnet.\nIts magnetic poles\nare NOT the same\nas the geographic\npoles.\nFigure 7.. Earth’s magnetic field .. From. Sear's & Zemansky's University Physics With Modern Physics (pp 884) by Young, H. D.,\nFreedman, R. A. & A. Lewis Ford(2016). England: Pearson.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "In 1820, Hans Christian Oersted  \nfound that when a compass  \nneedle is placed near an electric  \nwire, the needle deflects as  soon \nas the wire is connected to  a \nbattery and the wire carries an  \nelectric current.\nThe experiment showed that an  \nelectric current produces a  \nmagnetic field.\nFigure 8. Deflection of compass needles near a current- carrying wire, showing the presence and direction of the magnetic \nfield. From. https://vercauterendavid.files.wordpress.com/2017/12/chapter-27.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "Right hand rule (RHR) is used to  \ndetermine the direction of the magnetic  \nfield (B) relative t the current (I).\nFigure 9. 8agnetic field lines around an \nelectric current in a straight wire . From. \nhttps://vercauterendavid.files.wordpress.\ncom/2017/12/chapter-27.pdf\nFigure 10. Right-hand rule for remembering \nthe direction of the magnetic field . From. \nhttps://vercauterendavid.files.wordpress.com\n/2017/12/chapter-27.pdf\nFigure 11. Right hand grip rule. From.. \nhttp://bp0.blogger.com/_PyvjcvYfRLk/SFnp\nDI55a4I/AAAAAAAAAEQ/hiFS_Z2Nwk0/s40\n0/Right-Hand-Grip-Rules.png\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "Magnet exerts a force on a current-carrying wire. Direction of force is determined by  \nright-hand rule.\nFigure 12. a)Force on an electric current in a magnetic field .b) same, but current reversed; c) right-hand rule for setup in b). From. https://vercauterendavid.files.wordpress.com/2017/12/chapter-27.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "Where\nF = Force (N)\nI = current (A)\nl = length of wire exposed to magnetic field(m)\nB = magnetic field, tesla (T),\n1T = 1N/Am = 1 weber per meter squared =1 Wb/m2 = 104 gauss = 104G\nθ = angle between the current (I) direction and the magnetic field(B)\nThe force on the wire depends on the current, the length of wire, the magnetic  \nfield and the orientation. The magnetic force is perpendicular to the magnetic field  \ndirection.\nMaximum force occurs when θ =900.\nForce is zero if wire is parallel to fieldlines.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "1.  A straight horizontal copper rod carries current of 5.0 A from west to east in \na region between the poles of an electromagnet. In this region there is a \nhorizontal magnetic field toward the north east (450 north of east) with \nmagnitude 1.20 T. Find the magnitude of the force on the wire with a length of \n1.0 m rod.\nAnswer: 4.243 N\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "There are four key characteristics of the magnetic force on a moving charge. \n1.\nThe magnitude of the magnetic force is proportional to the magnitude of \nthe charge and to the speed v of the particle.\n2.\nThe magnitude of force is also proportional to the magnitude or “strength” \nof  the magnetic field.\n3.\nThe magnetic force is proportional to sinϴ.\n4. The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the magnetic field and the \nvelocity, that is, perpendicular to the plane formed by ⃑𝑣𝑎𝑛𝑑𝐵\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "The force on charged particles due to a  \nmagnetic field is perpendicular to the  \nmagnetic field direction.\nRight-hand rule is used to determine  the \ndirection of force. It is only true for  \npositively charged particles. For  \nnegatively charged particle, the force is  \nthe opposite direction.\nFigure 13.. Right hand rule to determine the direction of force. \nFrom. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/ch20_giancoli7.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "Where\nF = Force (N)  \nq = charge (C)\nv = velocity of charge(m/s)\nB = magnetic field, tesla (T),\n1T = 1N/Am = 1 weber per meter squared =1 Wb/m2 = 104 gauss = 104G\nθ = angle between the current (I) direction and the magnetic field(B)\nThe force on the wire depends on the current, the length of wire, the magnetic  \nfield and the orientation. The magnetic force is perpendicular to the magnetic field  \ndirection.\nMaximum force occurs when θ =900.\nForce is zero if particle moves parallel to fieldlines.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "Table 1. Summary of Right-hand rule. From.  http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/ch20_giancoli7.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "Figure 13. Alternative right-hand rule. From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics(pp 872) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "An electron in an old-style television picture \ntube moves toward the front of the tube \nwith a speed of 8.0 x 106 m/s along the x \naxis . Surrounding the neck of the tube are \ncoils of wire that create a magnetic field of \nmagnitude 0.025 T, directed at an angle of \n60° to the x axis and lying in the xy plane. \nCalculate the magnetic force on the electron.\nAnswer: 2.8  x 10 -14  N  \nFigure 14. Electron moving in an electric field. From. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with\nModern Physics(pp 874) by Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Australia: Cengage Learning.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nGiancoli, Douglas C. (2007). Physics 6th Edition.Jurong,  Singapore. \nPearson Education South Asia Pte Ltd.\nhttps://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.35wltD4EAIq_oicACd5gdAHaEo&\npid=Api&P=0&w=262&h=164\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "https://www.polytechnichub.com/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2018/08/magnetic-flux-lines.jpg\nhttps://tse2.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.JYb8OGDWupgs93Ey2e8L6QAAA\nA&pid=Api&P=0&w=213&h=177\nhttp://bp0.blogger.com/_PyvjcvYfRLk/SFnpDI55a4I/AAAAAAAAAEQ/\nhiFS_Z2Nwk0/s400/Right-Hand-Grip-Rules.png\nhttps://www.slideserve.com/Jims/\nhttp://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/ch20_giancoli7.pdf\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "Torque on a Current \nLoop\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§ Explain how current loops behave when placed in a magnetic field\n§ Solve problems involving magnetic field.\n§ Explain why currents in the same direction attract while wires carrying opposing currents repel\n§ Discuss Ampere’s Law and what it tells about magnetic fields.\n§ Use Ampere’s Law to calculate the magnetic field of symmetric current distributions.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "The two forces on the loop have equal magnitude but an application\nof RHR-1 shows that they are opposite in direction.\nFigure 15. Torque on a current-carrying coil. From. https://image.slidesharecdn.com/ch21magneticfieldsandforces23july2015-\n161130010302/95/ch21-magnetic-fields-and-forces-23-july-2015-39-638.jpg?cb=1480467828\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "The forces on opposite \nsides  of a current loop will \nbe equal  and opposite (if \nthe field is  uniform and the \nloop is  symmetric) but \nthere may be a  torque.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "The rectangular loop carries a current I \nin a  uniform magnetic field.\nNo magnetic force acts on sides 1 and 3\nMagnitude of the magnetic force on sides \n2  & 4, F = IAB\nThe direction of F2 is out of the page.  \nThe direction of F4 is into the page.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "A circular coil of wire has a diameter of 20.0 cm and contains 10 loops. The current in  \neach loop is 3.00 A, and the coil is placed in a 2.00 T external magnetic field.\nDetermine the maximum and minimum torque exerted on the coil by the field.\nSolution:\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "A galvanometer consists of a coil of  \nwire (with attached pointer)  \nsuspended in the magnetic field of a  \npermanent magnet. When the  \ncurrent flows through the loop of  \nwire, the magnetic field exerts a  \ntorque on the loop.\nGalvanometer is the basic  \ncomponent of analog meters  \nincluding ammeters, voltmeters and  \nohmmeters.\nFigure 16.. Galvanometer .  From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/ch20_giancoli7.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "An electric motor changes electric  \nenergy into rotational mechanical  \nenergy. It works on the same principle  \nas the galvanometer, except that there  \nis no spring so the coil can rotate  \ncontinuously in one direction.\nDiagram of a simple dc motor.\nFigure 17. Right Simple DC motor. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/ch20_giancoli7.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "text": "A loudspeaker works on the principle  \nthat a magnet exerts a force on a  \ncurrent-carrying wire. The electrical  \noutput of a stereo or TV set is  \nconnected to the wire leads of the  \nspeaker. The speaker leads are  \nconnected internally to a coil of wire,  \nwhich is itself attached to the speaker  \ncone.\nFigure 18.. Loudspeaker. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/ch20_giancoli7.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "text": "Magnetic field is inversely  \nproportional to the distance from  \nthe wire.\nWhere\nB = magnetic field, tesla (T),\nI = current,A\nr = distance from the wire (m)\nμ0 = permeability of free space(Tm/A)\nμ0 = 4π x 10-7Tm/A\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "text": "1. An electric wire in the wall of a  \nbuilding carries a DC current of 25 A  \nvertically upward. What is the  \nmagnetic field due to this current at  a \npoint P 10 cm due north of the  wire?\nP\n10cm\nI\nDirection can be determined using right hand \nrule.  Direction of B is to the west into thepage.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "text": "Figure 19. a) two parallel conductors carrying currents, I1  and I2, b) the magnetic \nfield B1 produced by I1. B1  points into page at position of I2. From.  \nhttps://image.slidesharecdn.com/ppa6lecturech20-100424010240-\nphpapp02/95/ppa6-lecture-ch20-20-728.jpg?cb=1272071064\nThe magnetic field produced at the position of \nwire 2 due to the current wire 1: \nThe force this field exerts on a length of wire 2 is: \n", "image": "slides/m8-main/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "text": "Parallel currents in the same direction exert an attractive  \nforce on each other.\nAntiparallel currents (opposite directions) exert a repulsive  \nforce on each other.\nFigure 20. a) parallel currents in the same direction, b) antiparallel currents . From.  \nhttps://image.slidesharecdn.com/ppa6lecturech20-100424010240-phpapp02/95/ppa6-\nlecture-ch20-20-728.jpg?cb=1272071064\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "text": "2. The 2 wires of a 2m long appliance cord are 3.0 mm apart and carry a current of 8.0 A  \ndc. Calculate the force one wire exerts on the other.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/036.webp"}, {"page": 37, "text": "A solenoid is a long coil of wire consisting of many loops or turns. If tightly wrapped,  the \nmagnetic field in its interior is almost uniform.\nIt acts like a magnet. One end can be considered the north pole while the other end is  the \nsouth pole depending on the direction of current in the loops.\nWhere \nN = number of turns\nI = current\nl =length of wire\nFigure 21. a) Solenoid . From.  https://image.slidesharecdn.com/ppa6lecturech20-100424010240-phpapp02/95/ppa6-lecture-ch20-20-\n728.jpg?cb=1272071064\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/037.webp"}, {"page": 38, "text": "If a piece of iron is place inside a solenoid,  the \nmagnetic field is increased greatly  because \nthe iron becomes a magnet. The  magnetic \nfield is the sum of that due to  current and that \ndue to iron and it can be  hundreds or \nthousands of times that due to  the current. \nSuch an an iron-core solenoid is  called\nelectromagnet.\nApplications include motors and generators.  \nOne simple application of electromagnet is  \ndoorbell.\nFigure 22. Iron-core solenoid . From.  https://image.slidesharecdn.com/ppa6lecturech20-\n100424010240-phpapp02/95/ppa6-lecture-ch20-20-728.jpg?cb=1272071064\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/038.webp"}, {"page": 39, "text": "3. A thin 12 cm long solenoid has a total of 420 turns of wire and carries a current of  2.0A. \nCalculate the field inside near the center.\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/039.webp"}, {"page": 40, "text": "The sum of all the products  \nof the the length of each  \nsegment and the  component \nof magnetic  field parallel to \nthe segment  is equal to the \nu0 times the  current that \npasses  through the surface  \nenclosed by the path.\nFigure 23. Ampere’s Law. From.  \nhttps://image.slidesharecdn.com/ppa6lecturec\nh20-100424010240-phpapp02/95/ppa6-\nlecture-ch20-20-728.jpg?cb=1272071064\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/040.webp"}, {"page": 41, "text": "Field due to straight wire:\nBecause of symmetry, all Bi’s are the\nsame.\nFigure 24. Field due to straight wire. From.  \nhttps://image.slidesharecdn.com/ppa6lecturech20-100424010240-\nphpapp02/95/ppa6-lecture-ch20-20-728.jpg?cb=1272071064\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/041.webp"}, {"page": 42, "text": "Field inside a solenoid:\nFigure 25. Field inside a solenoid. From.  https://image.slidesharecdn.com/ppa6lecturech20-\n100424010240-phpapp02/95/ppa6-lecture-ch20-20-728.jpg?cb=1272071064\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/042.webp"}, {"page": 43, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nGiancoli, Douglas C. (2007). Physics 6th Edition. Jurong,  Singapore.\nPearson Education South Asia Pte Ltd.\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/043.webp"}, {"page": 44, "text": "https://image.slidesharecdn.com/ch21magneticfieldsandforces23july\n2015-161130010302/95/ch21-magnetic-fields-and-forces-23-july-\n2015-39-638.jpg?cb=1480467828\nhttps://image.slidesharecdn.com/ppa6lecturech20-100424010240-\nphpapp02/95/ppa6-lecture-ch20-25-728.jpg?cb=1272071064\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m8-main/044.webp"}]}, {"id": "m9-main", "module": 9, "title": "Electromagnetic induction", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 9 - Physics.pdf", "sha256": "36604a54e426b2be7770f4f020b4593bda6045d92002034b239c29c351be5db0", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "ELECTROMAGNETIC \nINDUCTION \nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Magnetic Flux &\nFaraday’s Law Induction\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDiscuss how a changing magnetic flux generates an electric field \n§\nDescribe the quantities used in electromagnetism\n§\nDemonstrate how generator converts mechanical energy to electrical energy\n§\nExplain how Faraday’s law relates the induced emf in a loop to the change in  magnetic flux \nthrough the loop.\n§\nCalculate the emf induced in a conductor moving through a magnetic field\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Electromagnetic Induction is the process of using magnetic fields \nto produce voltage, and in a complete circuit, a current. \nMichael Faraday first discovered it, using some of the works of Hans \nChristian Oersted. His work started at first using different \ncombinations of wires and magnetic strengths and currents, but it \nwasn't until he tried moving the wires that he got any success. \nIt turns out that electromagnetic induction is created by just that - the \nmoving of a conductive substance through a magnetic field. \nFigure 1. Michael Faraday. From. \nhttps://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.39_6BXbofVe\nE_0AXgvYFwgAAAA&pid=Api&P=0&w=300&h=30\n0\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "American Joseph Henry and  \nEnglishman Michael Faraday  \nindependently found that it is possible  \nthat a magnetic field can produce an  \nelectric current.\nHenry made the discovery first but  \nFaraday published his results earlier  \nand investigated the subject in more  \ndetail.\nFigure 2. Induced emf. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch21_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "A coil of wire X was connected to a battery.  \nThe current that flows through X produced a  \nmagnetic field that was intensified by the  iron \ncore around which the wire was  wrapped.\nThe 2nd  circuitY contained\na galvanometer  \nto detect any current but contained no  \nbattery.\nA constant current in X produced a constant  \nmagnetic field which produced NO  CURRENT \nin Y. Only when the current in X  was starting \nor stopping was a current  produced in Y.\nX\nY\nFigure 2. Induced emf. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch21_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "Faraday concluded that although a  \nconstant magnetic field produces no  \ncurrent in a conductor, a changing \nmagnetic field can produce an electric \ncurrent. Such current is called an  \ninduced current. When the magnetic  \nfield through coil Y changes, a current  \noccurs in Y as if there were a source of  \nemf in circuit Y.\nA changing magnetic field induces an\nemf.\nMotion or change is required to induce  \nemf. It does not matter whether the  \nmagnet or coil moves.\nFigure 3. Changing magnetic field. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch21_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "The induced emf in a wire loop is proportional to the rate of change  \nof the magne tic flux, ΦB, through the loop.\nWhere\nΦB = magnetic flux, Weber (Wb) (1 Wb= 1 Tm2)\nB⊥= component of the magnetic field B  \nperpendicular to the face of the loop, T\nB = uniform magnetic field, Tesla (T)\nA = area of the loop, square meter (m2)\nθ = angle between B and a line perpendicular to\nthe face of the loop\nFigure 4. Changing magnetic field. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch21_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "Magnetic flux is proportional to the total  \nnumber of lines passing through the loop\nWays to change the magnetic flux:\n-Changing the magnitude of the field within a  \nconducting loop\n-Changing the area of the loop that lies within  \nthe magnetic field\n-Changing the relative orientation of the field  \nand the loop\nFigure 5. Changing magnetic field. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch21_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "1. A square loop of wire encloses area A1. A uniform magneticfield B perpendicular to  the loop \nextends over the are A2. What is the magnetic flux through the loopA1?\nA2\nA1\nWire loop\n0\nThere is no magnetic field (B=0) in the area (A1-A2),  so \nflux is zero in that area. Magnetic flux through  loop A1 \nis not BA1 because B is NOT UNIFORM over  A1.\nFigure 6. Square loop of wire. From. https://image1.slideserve.com/3566120/magnetic-flux3-l.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "2. A square loop of wire 10.0 cm on a side is in a 1.25 T magnetic field B. What are the  \nmaximum and minimum values of flux that can pass through the loop?\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "One of the basic laws of electromagnetism\nFaraday’s law of  \ninduction states that  \nthe emf induced in a  \ncircuit is equal to the  \nrate of change of  \nmagnetic flux through \nthe circuit.\nIf the circuit contains N closely wrapped  \nloops, the emfs induced in each loop add  \ntogether.\n• The minus sign gives the direction of the induced\nemf.\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "Faraday learned that if you change any\npart of the flux over\ntime you could\ninduce a current in a conductor and thus\ncreate a source of EMF (voltage, potential\ndifference).\nSince we are dealing with time here we\nare talking about the RATE of CHANGE of\nFLUX, which is called\nFaraday’s Law.\nFigure 7. Rate of change of flux. From. http://1.bp.blogspot.com/-\n73rqafR5TMs/UbRNfY9uChI/AAAAAAAAFsg/BmvOWfJ58gI/s640/Faraday\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "AC Generators use Faraday’s  \nlaw to produce rotation and  \nthus convert electrical and  \nmagnetic energy into  rotational \nkinetic energy.\nThis idea can be used to  run \nall kinds of motors.\nSince the current in the coil  is \nAC, it is turning on and\noff thus creating a  CHANGING\nmagnetic field  of its own. Its \nown  magnetic field interferes\nwith the shown magnetic  \nfield to produce rotation.\nAPPLICATION\nFigure 8. AC generator. From. https://www.splung.com/fields/images/induction/acgen.gif\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "A microphone works when \nsound  waves enter the filter of a  \nmicrophone. Inside the filter, a  \ndiaphragm is vibrated by the  \nsound waves which in turn \nmoves  a coil of wire wrapped \naround a  magnet.\nThe movement of the wire  in \nthe magnetic field induces a  \ncurrent in the wire. \nThus sound  waves can be \nturned into  electronic signals \nand then  amplified through a\nspeaker.\nAPPLICATION\nFigure 9. Microphone diaphragm. From.  http://www.pjaviation.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2012/05/image001.gif\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "Probably one of the greatest inventions of all time is the  transformer. AC \nCurrent from the primary coil moves quickly  BACK and FORTH (thus the idea \nof changing!) across the  secondary coil. The moving magnetic field caused by \nthe  changing field (flux) induces a current in the secondary coil.\nIf the secondary coil has MORE turns  \nthan the primary you can step up the  \nvoltage and runs devices that would  \nnormally need MORE voltage than  \nwhat you have coming in. We call this  \na STEP UP transformer.\nWe can use this idea in reverse as well  \nto create a STEP DOWN transformer.\nAPPLICATION\nFigure 10. Transformer. From. https://www.scienceabc.com/wp-content/uploads/ext-www.scienceabc.com/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2018/06/step-down-transformer.jpg-.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "From Faraday’s law, the emf in the secondary and primary coil is\nwhere\nNs is the number or turns in the\nsecondary coil\nNp is the number or turns in the  \nsecondary coil\n∆ϕ/∆t is the rate at which the  \nmagnetic flux changes\nTRANSFORMER\nFigure 10. Transformer. From. https://www.scienceabc.com/wp-content/uploads/ext-www.scienceabc.com/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2018/06/step-down-transformer.jpg-.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "1.\nA coil with 200 turns of wire is wrapped on an 18.0 cm square frame. Each turn has\nthe same area, equal to that of the frame, and the total resistance of the coil is 2.0Ω .\nA uniform magnetic field is applied perpendicularly to the plane of the coil. If the\nfield\nchanges uniformly from 0 to 0.500 T in 0.80 s, find the magnitude\nof the\ninduced emf in the coil while the field has changed as well as the magnitude of the\ninduced current.\nAnswer: emf = 4.1 V\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "2. A transformer for home use of a portable radio reduces 120-V ac to\n9.0-V dc. A\nsecondary coil contains 30 turns and the radio draws 400mA. Calculate a) the number of\nturns in the primary coil, b) the current in the primary and c) the power transformed\nAnswer:\na) 400 turns\nb) 0.03 A\nc) 3.6 W\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nGiancoli, Douglas C. (2007). Physics 6th Edition.Jurong,  Singapore. \nPearson Education South Asia Pte Ltd.\nhttps://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.39_6BXbofVeE_0AXgvYFwgAAAA\n&pid=Api&P=0&w=300&h=300\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.39_6BXbofVeE_0AXgvYFwgAAAA\n&pid=Api&P=0&w=300&h=300\nhttps://image1.slideserve.com/3566120/magnetic-flux3-l.jpg\nhttp://www.pjaviation.co.za/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2012/05/image001.gif\nhttps://www.scienceabc.com/wp-content/uploads/ext-\nwww.scienceabc.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/step-down-\ntransformer.jpg-.jpg\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Lenz’s Law, Motional EMF, \nInductance & RL Circuits\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDetermine the direction of an induced emf.\n§\nCalculate the emf induced in a conductor moving through a magnetic field.\n§\nDiscuss how a changing magnetic flux generates an electric field that is very different from that\nproduced by an arrangement of charges.\n§\nAnalyze circuits that include both a resistor and an inductor.\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "Lenz’s law states that \na current produced by \nan induced emf moves \nin a direction so that \nits magnetic field \nopposes the original \nchange in the flux.\nLenz’s law is used to determine the direction \nof the (conventional) electric current \ninduced in a loop due to a change in \nmagnetic flux inside the loop. \nTo produce an induced current, you need:\na.\nA closed conducting loop\nb.   An external magnetic flux through the \nloop that is changing in time\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "How to determine the direction of the induced current?\n1.\nDetermine whether the magnetic flux (Φ = BAcosθ) inside the loop is decreasing, increasing,  or\nunchanged.\n2.\nThe magnetic field due to the induced current (Binduced):\na. points in the SAME direction as the external field if the flux is DECREASING\nb. points in the OPPOSITE direction as the external field if the flux is INCREASING\nc. ZERO if the flux is not changing.\n3. Once you know the direction of the induced magnetic field, use the right hand rule (thumb & curl\nof fingers for B and I) to find the direction of the induced current.\n4. Always keep in mind that there are 2 magnetic fields.\n1. an external field whose flux must be changing if it is to induce an electric current, and\n2. a magnetic field produced by the induced current.\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "Magnetic field of induced current opposes change in external flux, NOT necessarily  \nopposing the external field.\nFigure 11. Lenz’s Law. From. https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.xC6-glhcGg2ebKB7vRH5BAHaFj&pid=Api&P=0&w=259&h=195\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "1. What is the direction of the induced current in the circular loop due to the current  \nshown in the figure.\nI increasing\nUsing\nRHR:\nthumb – current (I) direction (to the left)\ncurl of fingers – direction of magnetic field (B) in the loop is\nINWARD\nSince the current (I) is increasing, the magnetic field (B) is also increasing. \nThus, the  direction the induced magnetic field (Bind) is OUTWARD (towards \nyou). (Rule: The  magnetic field due to the induced current (Binduced) points in \nthe OPPOSITE direction as  the external field if the flux is INCREASING)\nUsing RHR\nagain:\nthumb – induced magnetic field (Bind) in OUTWARD\ndirection\ncurl of fingers –induced current (Iind) direction which is\nCOUNTERCLOCKWISE\nFigure 12. Sample  1 Lenz’s Law. From. \nhttp://d2vlcm61l7u1fs.cloudfront.net/media\n%2F502%2F502b46b0-933e-4374-b0e2-\nc2ba6eef9411%2FphpquDpjb.png\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "2. What is the direction of the induced current in the circular loop due to the current  \nshown in the figure.\nI decreasing\nCurrent (I) is to the LEFT\nMagnetic field (B) in the loop is\nINWARD\nInduced magnetic field (Bind) is\nOUTWARD\nInduced current (Iind) is\nCOUNTERCLOCKWISE\nI\nB\nB\nBin\nd\nIin\nd\nFigure 13. Sample 2 Lenz’s Law. From. \nhttp://d2vlcm61l7u1fs.cloudfront.net/media%2F5\n02%2F502b46b0-933e-4374-b0e2-\nc2ba6eef9411%2FphpquDpjb.png\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "3. What is the direction of the induced current in the circular loop due to the current  \nshown in the figure.\nI decreasing\nUsing RHR:\nthumb – current (I) in UPWARD direction\ncurl of fingers – direction of magnetic field (B) in the loop is\nINWARD\nSince the current (I) is decreasing, the magnetic field (B) is also decreasing.\nThus, the direction the induced magnetic field (Bind) is ALSO INWARD (into\nthe page). (Rule: The\nmagnetic field due to the induced current (Binduced)\npoints in the SAME direction as the external field if the flux is DECREASING.)\nUsing RHR\nagain:\nthumb – induced magnetic field (Bind) in INWARD direction\ncurl of fingers –induced current (Iind) direction which is\nCLOCKWISE\nFigure 14. Sample 3 Lenz’s Law. From. \nhttp://d2vlcm61l7u1fs.cloudfront.net/media%\n2F502%2F502b46b0-933e-4374-b0e2-\nc2ba6eef9411%2FphpquDpjb.png\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "4. What is the direction of the induced current in the circular loop due to the current  \nshown in the figure.\nI decreasing\nCurrent (I) is UPWARD\nMagnetic field (B) in the loop is\nINWARD\nInduced magnetic field (Bind) is ALSO\nINWARD\ninduced current (Iind) is CLOCKWISE\nI\nB\nB\nBin\nd\nIin\nd\nFigure 15. Sample 4 Lenz’s Law. From. \nhttp://d2vlcm61l7u1fs.cloudfront.net/media%2F502\n%2F502b46b0-933e-4374-b0e2-\nc2ba6eef9411%2FphpquDpjb.png\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "text": "A uniform magnetic field B is  \nperpendicular to the area bounded by  \nthe U-shaped conductor and the  \nmovable rod resting on it.\nIf rod is made to move at a speed of v, it  \ntravels a distance of Δx = vt in a timeΔt.  \nThe area of the loop increases by an  \namount ΔA=lΔx = lvΔt in a time Δt. By  \nFaraday’s law, there is an induced emf  \nwhose magnitude is:\nFigure 16. A conducting rod is moved to the right on a U-shaped  conductor in a uniform magnetic field B that points out of the  paper. \nFrom. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch21_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "text": "Equation is valid as long as  \nB, l, v are mutually  \nperpendicular.\nAn emf induced on\na  \nconductor by moving in a  \nmagnetic field is called  \nmotional emf.\nFigure 16. A conducting rod is moved to the right on a U-shaped  conductor in a uniform magnetic field B that points out of the  paper. \nFrom. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch21_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "text": "The induced current is in a direction that tends to slow the moving bar. It takes an  \nexternal force to keep it moving.\nFigure 17. Induced current tends to slow the moving bar. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch21_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/033.webp"}, {"page": 34, "text": "A changing magnetic field induces an electric field.\nThis applies not only to wires and other conductors, but\na general result that applies  \nto any region in space: an electric field will be induced at any point in space where  there \nis a changing magnetic field.\nFor the case of electrons in a moving conductor, a simple formula for E in terms of B.  The \nelectric field in the rod is:\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/034.webp"}, {"page": 35, "text": "5. An airplane travels 1000km/h in a region where the Earth’s magnetic field is 5.0 x10^-\n5 T and is nearly vertical. What is the potential difference induced between the wing  \ntips that are 70m apart? (Consider the wings to be a 70m long conductor moving  \nthrough the Earth’s magnetic field.) \nSolution:\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/035.webp"}, {"page": 36, "text": "Eddy currents can dramatically slow a  \nconductor moving into or out of a magnetic  \nfield.\nEddy currents are  \ninduced currents  \nflowing in bulk  \nmaterial as well  \nthrough wires.\nFigure 18. Production of Eddy currents in a rotating wheel. From. http://image.slideserve.com/228996/eddy-current-l.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/036.webp"}, {"page": 37, "text": "Mutual Inductance\nMutual inductance is the  \nphenomenon in which a  \nchange in current in one  \ncoil causes an induced  \ncurrent in another coil  \nplaced near to the first coil.\nFigure 19. Mutual Inductance. From. https://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.QtNMipESQn7FAEsvcWx0cwHaEs&pid=Api&P=0&w=241&h=154\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/037.webp"}, {"page": 38, "text": "Where\nM = mutual inductance, henry (H)  \n1H = 1 Vs/A = 1 Ωs\nI =current,A  \nt = times, s\nξ =emf\nM does not depend on I1. It depends on the geometric  \nfactors such as size, shape, number of turns and  \nrelative positions of the 2 coils and also on whether  \niron (or other ferromagnetic material) is present.\nApplications:\n-transformers\n-pacemakers\nFigure 19. Mutual Inductance. From. https://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.QtNMipESQn7FAEsvcWx0cwHaEs&pid=Api&P=0&w=241&h=154\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/038.webp"}, {"page": 39, "text": "If the current in the coil is changing, the  \nchanging flux through the coil induces an  \nemf in the coil.\nWher\ne\nL = self-inductance, henry (H)\n1H = 1 Vs/A = 1 Ωs\nI =current, A  \nt = times, s\nξ =emf\nFigure 20. Inductance. From. https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.3a0mzli2W76Cq5UXIY33CgHaF9&pid=Api&P=0&w=222&h=179\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/039.webp"}, {"page": 40, "text": "SELF-INDUCTANCE\nThe magnitude of L depends on the size and\nthe shape of the coil and on the presence of\nan iron core.\nAn ac circuit always contains some inductance, \nbut  quite small unless the circuit contains a coil \nof many  loops or turns.\nA coil that has a significant \nself- inductance (L) is called \nan  inductor.\nFigure 22. Inductor  symbol.From.  https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.-\nW_WoN1m90JCK3ZwNtvqnQAAAA&pid=Api&P=0&w=300&h=300\nFigure 21. Several inductors. From.\nhttps://tse3.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.AMreBf6ibGzO1lf6WQFeLg\nHaGk&pid=Api&P=0&w=184&h=164\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/040.webp"}, {"page": 41, "text": "The direction of a self-induced emf is found rom Lenz’s law. The cause of the emf  is \nan increasing or decreasing in current.\nFigure 23. Direction of self-induced emf. From. https://tse3.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.HXFyxSzuuOeXceDbfFbNEgHaDm&pid=Api&P=0&w=316&h=154\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/041.webp"}, {"page": 42, "text": "Any inductor will have resistance. It is  \nrepresented by drawing the inductance L and  \nthe resistance R separately. The resistance R  \ncould also include a separate resistor  \nconnected in series.\nWhen the switch is turned on (closed), the  \ncurrent starts to flow. It is opposed by the  \ninduced emf in the inductor because of the  \nchanging current. However, as soon as the  \ncurrent starts to flow, there is a voltage drop  \nacross the resistance (V=IR).\nFigure 24. RL circuit. From. https://electronicscoach.com/wp-\ncontent/uploads/2017/12/RL-circuit.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/042.webp"}, {"page": 43, "text": "LR Circuit with emf\nLR Circuit without emf\nThe time constant (τ) is the time for the current to decrease to 37% of the original value.  \nThe time constant is inversely proportional to R.\nFigure 25. Transient time .From. https://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.H5IrzNcRLvtdvJfQL2nCqQAAAA&pid=Api&P=0&w=256&h=148\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/043.webp"}, {"page": 44, "text": "A solenoid has an inductance of 87.5mH and a resistance of 0.250Ω. Find (a) the time  \nconstant for this circuit, and (b) how long it would take for the current to go from zero  \nto 63% of its final (maximum) value when connected to a battery of voltage, V.\nSolution:\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/044.webp"}, {"page": 45, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nGiancoli, Douglas C. (2007). Physics 6th Edition.Jurong,  Singapore. \nPearson Education South Asia Pte Ltd.\nhttps://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.xC6-\nglhcGg2ebKB7vRH5BAHaFj&pid=Api&P=0&w=259&h=195\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/045.webp"}, {"page": 46, "text": "http://d2vlcm61l7u1fs.cloudfront.net/media%2F502%2F502b46b0-\n933e-4374-b0e2-c2ba6eef9411%2FphpquDpjb.png\nhttps://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.QtNMipESQn7FAEsvcWx0cwHaE\ns&pid=Api&P=0&w=241&h=154\nhttps://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.3a0mzli2W76Cq5UXIY33CgHaF9\n&pid=Api&P=0&w=222&h=179\nhttps://tse3.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.AMreBf6ibGzO1lf6WQFeLgHaGk\n&pid=Api&P=0&w=184&h=164\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/046.webp"}, {"page": 47, "text": "http://www.solitaryroad.com/c1048/ole12.gif\nhttps://electronicscoach.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/RL-\ncircuit.jpg\nhttps://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.H5IrzNcRLvtdvJfQL2nCqQAAAA&\npid=Api&P=0&w=256&h=148\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m9-main/047.webp"}]}, {"id": "m10-main", "module": 10, "title": "Light & optics", "variant": "main", "file": "Module 10 - Physics (1).pdf", "sha256": "c76d91cb913bd4ba99959277827ab3eadb18af323f3b112019a90b83176a4a2d", "pages": [{"page": 1, "text": "Lights and Optics\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/001.webp"}, {"page": 2, "text": "Properties of Light\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/002.webp"}, {"page": 3, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nExplain the nature of light\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/003.webp"}, {"page": 4, "text": "Like all waves, electromagnetic waves\nhave a wavelength and\nfrequency, related by:\nl\nf\nc =\nFigure 1. Electromagnetic spectrum. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch22_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/004.webp"}, {"page": 5, "text": "Thespeedatwhichalllight(all partsof\nthe electromagnetic spectrum)\ntravels in a\nvacuum.\nc=2.99792458x108m/s\nThe duration of one second is defined\nbythecesiumclock,soone meterisdefinedto be\nthedistancethat lighttravelsin1/299,792,458 s.\nFigure 2. Cesium clock. From. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodern\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/005.webp"}, {"page": 6, "text": "Before 19th century:\nLight was considered to be a stream of particles. The  \nparticles were either emitted by the object being viewed or  \nemanated from the eyes of the viewer. Newton was the chief  \narchitect of the particle theory of light. He believed the particles  \nleft the object and stimulated the sense of sight upon entering the  \neyes.\nDuring the 19th century:\nOther developments led to the general acceptance of the  \nwave theory of light. Christian Huygens argued that light might be  \nsome sort of a wave motion. Thomas Young (in 1801) provided the  \nfirst clear demonstration of the wave nature of light. He showed  that \nlight rays interfere with each other. Such behavior could not be  \nexplained by particles.\nDual nature of Light\nFigure 3. Light as particles and wave. From. \nhttps://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.b1Zx6s1iOWsZoDZQhD0kwgHaGM&pid=\nApi&P=0&w=202&h=170\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/006.webp"}, {"page": 7, "text": "• Propagation of light is bestdescribed by a wavemodel.\n• Can be expressed in any unit of length,  usually in meters \n(m) and nanometer  (nm).\n• Represented by lambda(λ).\nFigure 4. Wavelength of light. From.  https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/8879/waves.png?revision=1&size=bestfit&width=342&height=200\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/007.webp"}, {"page": 8, "text": "Find the range in wavelengths for visible light in the frequency \nrange between 4.0x1014Hz and 7.9x1014Hz\nSolution:\nnm\n \n750\nm\n10\n5.7\nHz\n10\n4.0\ns\nm\n10\n00\n.3\n7\n14\n8\n=\n´\n=\n´\n´\n=\n=\n-\nf\nc\nl\nnm\n \n380\nm\n10\n8.3\nHz\n10\n7.9\ns\nm\n10\n00\n.3\n7\n14\n8\n=\n´\n=\n´\n´\n=\n=\n-\nf\nc\nl\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/008.webp"}, {"page": 9, "text": "Figure 4. Nature of light. From. https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.JhBqInK-GvH66ys5KQKItQHaCs&pid=Api&P=0&w=479&h=175\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/009.webp"}, {"page": 10, "text": "The photoelectric effect was a major phenomenon not explained by waves.\nWhen light strikes a metal surface, electrons are sometimes ejected from the surface.  \nThe kinetic energy of the ejected electron is independent of the frequency of the light.\nEinstein (in 1905) proposed an explanation of the photoelectric effect that used the idea of  \nquantization.\nThe quantization model assumes that the energy of a light wave is present in particles called  \nphotons.\nE = hƒ\nwhere h is Planck’s Constant and = 6.63 x 10-34 J.s\nIn view of these developments, light must be regarded as having a dual nature.\nLight exhibits the characteristics of a wave in some situations and the characteristics of a  \nparticle in other situations.\nFor practical applications of geometric optics, light is treated as a wave phenomenon.\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/010.webp"}, {"page": 11, "text": "Ray optics (sometimes called geometric optics) involves the study of the propagation  of\nlight.\nIt uses the assumption that light travels in a straight-line path in a uniform medium  \nand changes its direction when it meets the surface of a different medium or if the  \noptical properties of the medium are non-uniform.\nThe ray approximation is used to represent beams of light.\nFigure 5. Ray optics. From. https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.MqTe-1fSg7VRhVO_DlHuJAHaDe&pid=Api&P=0&w=436&h=205\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/011.webp"}, {"page": 12, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nGiancoli, Douglas C. (2007). Physics 6th Edition.Jurong,  Singapore. \nPearson Education South Asia Pte Ltd.\nhttp://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch22_Giancoli7e.pdf\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/012.webp"}, {"page": 13, "text": "https://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.b1Zx6s1iOWsZoDZQhD0kwgHaG\nM&pid=Api&P=0&w=202&h=170\nhttps://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/8879/waves.png?revisio\nn=1&size=bestfit&width=342&height=200\nhttps://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.MqTe-\n1fSg7VRhVO_DlHuJAHaDe&pid=Api&P=0&w=436&h=205\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/013.webp"}, {"page": 14, "text": "Reflection, Refraction, Snell’s \nLaw & Total Internal Reflection\nMPS Department | FEU Institute of  Technology\nCollege Physics 2\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/014.webp"}, {"page": 15, "text": "OBJECTIVES\n§\nDiscuss the Law of Reflection and Law of Refraction\n§\nExplain Snell’s Law\n§\nUse Snell’s law in determining angle of refraction\n§\nExplain Total Internal Reflection\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/015.webp"}, {"page": 16, "text": "The angle of  \nreflection is equal  \nto the angle of  \nincidence.\nFigure 6. Law of reflection. From. http://cnx.org/resources/55c2011fd4cdc676168cc52e7aadaff7/Figure%2026_02_01.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/016.webp"}, {"page": 17, "text": "The reflection of light can be roughly categorized into two \ntypes of reflection:\nSpecular reflection --- light reflected from a smooth surface \nat a definite angle.\nDiffuse reflection --- produced by rough surfaces that tend \nto reflect light in all directions. \n", "image": "slides/m10-main/017.webp"}, {"page": 18, "text": "Refraction is the  \nbending of the ray  \nof light as it passes  \nthe boundary of  \ntwo media.\nRefraction is caused by the change in speed  \nexperienced by a wave when it changes  \nmedium.\nFigure 7. Refraction. From. https://wiki.anton-paar.com/fileadmin/wiki/images/basics-of-refractometry/refraction-of-light.jpg\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/018.webp"}, {"page": 19, "text": "", "image": "slides/m10-main/019.webp"}, {"page": 20, "text": "", "image": "slides/m10-main/020.webp"}, {"page": 21, "text": "The incident ray, the refracted ray and the normal to\nthe surface separating the two media at the point of\nincidence, all lies on the same plane.\nWhen light travels from a medium n1 into another  \nmedium n2,\n- when n2 > n1, light will refract toward the normal.\n- when n1 > n2, light will refract away from the normal.\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/021.webp"}, {"page": 22, "text": "Angle of incidence  \nless than the  \ncritical angle.\nRefraction occurs.\nAngle of incidence  \nis equal to the  \ncritical angle. Light  \nis refracted along  \nthe boundary.\nAngle of \nincidence  greater \nthan the  critical \nangle. Total  \ninternal \nreflection  occurs.\nAngle of incidence  \nis 00 from normal,  \nLight enters the  \nnext medium at 00  \nfrom from normal  \nalso.\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/022.webp"}, {"page": 23, "text": "The angle of refraction depends on the  \nspeed of light in the two media and on the  \nincident angle.\nSnell’s Law is the basic of law of refraction.\nA ray of light bends in  \nsuch a way that the  \nratio of the sine of the  \nangle of incidence to  \nthe sine of the angle of  \nrefraction is constant.\nWhere\nn1= index of refraction for medium 1\nn2= index of refraction for medium 2\nθ1= angle ofincidence\nθ2 = angle ofrefraction\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/023.webp"}, {"page": 24, "text": "If light enters a medium where\nn is greater (its speed is less),\nthen the ray is bent toward the\nnormal.\nIf light enters a medium where  \nn is lesser (its speed is  higher), \nthen the ray is AWAY  from the\nnormal.\nFigure 8. Snell’s Law. From. http://hep0.okstate.edu/flera/phys1214/Ch22_Giancoli7e.pdf\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/024.webp"}, {"page": 25, "text": "1.  Light traveling in air strikes the \nwater  at an incident angle of 45°. \nWhat is the  angle of refraction?\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/025.webp"}, {"page": 26, "text": "2. Light passes through water then to a  \nthick glass and to air as illustrated.\nA.\nDetermine the angle of the reflected  \nlight in water, A.\nB.\nDetermine the angle of refraction of  \nlight in the glass, B.\nC.\nDetermine the angle of incidence of  \nlight as it exits the glass, C.\nD.\nDetermine the angle of refraction of  \nlight as it enters the air, D.\nD\nA\n20\nº\nglass\nH20\nn =1.3\nn =1.5\nB\nC\nair\nn =1\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/026.webp"}, {"page": 27, "text": "A.\nA = 200\nB.\nB =\n17.240\nA.\nC =\n17.240\nB.\nD = 200\nD\nA\n20\nº\nglass\n2\nH 0\nn =1.3\nn =1.5\nB\nC\nair\nn =1\nSolution\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/027.webp"}, {"page": 28, "text": "The critical angle is  \nthe angle of  \nincidence that  \nproduces an angle  \nof refraction of 90º\nFigure 9. Reflection and refraction. From. https://habibs.files.wordpress.com/2008/03/critical_angle.gif?w=544\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/028.webp"}, {"page": 29, "text": "Total internal reflection  \noccurs when light attempts  \nto pass from a more  \noptically dense medium to  a \nless optically dense  \nmedium at an angle greater  \nthan the critical angle.\nMedium 2 n2\nMedium 1 n1\nNo refraction, only\nreflection.\nFigure 10. Total Internal reflection. From. https://habibs.files.wordpress.com/2008/03/critical_angle.gif?w=544\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/029.webp"}, {"page": 30, "text": "Cladding has lower refractive index.  \nCore has higher refractive index.\nFigure 11. Fiber optic total internal reflections. From. https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.Jkc_QRWDu0Gd4GN5espOfAHaDL&pid=Api&P=0&w=380&h=164\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/030.webp"}, {"page": 31, "text": "Light travels through the core of a fiber optic  by continually reflecting off of the cladding.\nDue to total internal reflection, the cladding  does not absorb any of the light, allowing the  light to \ntravel over great distances.\nFiber optic lines are strands of glass or  transparent fibers that allows the transmission  of light and \ndigital information over long  distances. They are used for the telephone  system, the cable TV system, \nthe internet,  medical imaging, and mechanical engineering  inspection.\nFigure 12. Fiber optic lines with two light signals. From  \nhttps://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.QeU4_lp3S2PU1mNLmh3CtgAAAA&pid=Api&P=0&w=382&h=207\nFigure 13. Fiber cable. From. https://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.NW9Km9vq-\ntoUw5w5Q6OfsQHaEK&pid=Api&P=0&w=305&h=172\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/031.webp"}, {"page": 32, "text": "Serway, R. A., & Jewett, J. W. (2019). Physics for Scientists and \nEngineers with Modern Physics Tenth Edition. Australia: Cengage \nLearning.\nYoung, H. D., & Freedman, R. A. (2016). Sear's & Zemansky's University \nPhysics With Modern Physics. England: Pearson.\nGiancoli, Douglas C. (2007). Physics 6th Edition.Jurong,  Singapore. \nPearson Education South Asia Pte Ltd.\nhttp://cnx.org/resources/55c2011fd4cdc676168cc52e7aadaff7/Figur\ne%2026_02_01.jpg\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/032.webp"}, {"page": 33, "text": "https://wiki.anton-paar.com/fileadmin/wiki/images/basics-of-\nrefractometry/refraction-of-light.jpg\nhttps://habibs.files.wordpress.com/2008/03/critical_angle.gif?w=544\nhttps://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.Jkc_QRWDu0Gd4GN5espOfAHaDL&pi\nd=Api&P=0&w=380&h=164\nhttps://tse4.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.QeU4_lp3S2PU1mNLmh3CtgAAAA&pi\nd=Api&P=0&w=382&h=207\nhttps://tse1.mm.bing.net/th?id=OIP.NW9Km9vq-\ntoUw5w5Q6OfsQHaEK&pid=Api&P=0&w=305&h=172\nREFERENCES\n", "image": "slides/m10-main/033.webp"}]}];